var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"bvh_hit_tests_content.html#TLAS-Hit-Testing:-closest_hit-vs-any_hit","page":"TLAS Hit Testing: closest_hit vs any_hit","text":"This document tests and visualizes the difference between closest_hit and any_hit functions in the TLAS implementation using the trace_rays API.","title":"TLAS Hit Testing: closest_hit vs any_hit"},{"category":"section","location":"bvh_hit_tests_content.html#Test-Setup","page":"TLAS Hit Testing: closest_hit vs any_hit","text":"using Raycore, GeometryBasics, LinearAlgebra\nusing WGLMakie\nusing Test\nusing Bonito\nimport KernelAbstractions as KA\n\n# Create a simple test scene with multiple overlapping primitives\nfunction create_test_scene()\n    # Three spheres at different distances along the Z-axis\n    sphere1 = normal_mesh(Sphere(Point3f(0, 0, 5), 1.0f0))   # Furthest\n    sphere2 = normal_mesh(Sphere(Point3f(0, 0, 3), 1.0f0))   # Middle\n    sphere3 = normal_mesh(Sphere(Point3f(0, 0, 1), 1.0f0))   # Closest\n\n    tlas = Raycore.TLAS(KA.CPU())\n    push!(tlas, sphere1)\n    push!(tlas, sphere2)\n    push!(tlas, sphere3)\n    sync!(tlas)\n    return tlas\nend\n\ntlas = create_test_scene()","title":"Test Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"bvh_hit_tests_content.html#Test-1:-Single-Ray-Through-Center","page":"TLAS Hit Testing: closest_hit vs any_hit","text":"Test a ray through the center that passes through all three spheres.\n\n# Create a ray with slight offset to avoid hitting triangle vertices exactly\ntest_ray = Raycore.Ray(o=Point3f(0.1, 0.1, -5), d=Vec3f(0, 0, 1))\n\n# Trace with closest_hit (default)\nresult_closest = trace_rays(tlas, [test_ray])\n\nfig = Figure()\n\n# Left: closest_hit visualization\nplot(fig[1, 1], result_closest; axis=(; show_axis=false))\nLabel(fig[0, 1], \"closest_hit\", fontsize=20, font=:bold, tellwidth=false)\n\nfig","title":"Test 1: Single Ray Through Center"},{"category":"section","location":"bvh_hit_tests_content.html#Test-2:-Multiple-Rays-from-Different-Positions","page":"TLAS Hit Testing: closest_hit vs any_hit","text":"Test multiple rays to ensure both functions work correctly.\n\n# Test rays from different angles (with slight offset to avoid vertex hits)\ntest_positions = map(p-> (p = p.-0.5; Point3f(p..., -5)), rand(Point2f, 10))\n# Create rays\nrays = [Raycore.Ray(o=pos, d=Vec3f(0, 0, 1)) for pos in test_positions]\n\n# Trace rays and visualize\nresult_multi = trace_rays(tlas, rays)\nplot(result_multi; axis=(;show_axis=false))","title":"Test 2: Multiple Rays from Different Positions"},{"category":"section","location":"bvh_hit_tests_content.html#Test-3:-Complex-Scene","page":"TLAS Hit Testing: closest_hit vs any_hit","text":"Demonstrate ray tracing through a complex scene with many overlapping objects.\n\n# Create a complex scene with overlapping geometry\nusing Random\nRandom.seed!(123)\n\ncomplex_tlas = Raycore.TLAS(KA.CPU())\n\n# Add some large overlapping spheres\npush!(complex_tlas, normal_mesh(Sphere(Point3f(0, 0, 10), 3.0f0)))\npush!(complex_tlas, normal_mesh(Sphere(Point3f(0.5, 0, 5), 0.5f0)))\npush!(complex_tlas, normal_mesh(Sphere(Point3f(-0.5, 0, 15), 1.5f0)))\n\n# Add many small spheres to create complex TLAS structure\nfor i in 1:30\n    x = randn() * 5\n    y = randn() * 5\n    z = rand(8.0:0.5:12.0)\n    r = 0.3 + rand() * 0.5\n    push!(complex_tlas, normal_mesh(Sphere(Point3f(x, y, z), Float32(r))))\nend\n\nsync!(complex_tlas)\n\n# Test rays\ntest_rays = map(rand(Point2f, 20)) do p\n    p = (p .* 14f0) .- 8f0\n    Raycore.Ray(o=Point3f(p..., -5), d=Vec3f(0, 0, 1))\nend\n\nresult = trace_rays(complex_tlas, test_rays)\n\nfig = Figure()\nplot(fig[1, 1], result; axis=(; show_axis=false))\nLabel(fig[0, 1], \"closest_hit\", tellwidth=false)\n\nfig\n\n\nKey Findings:\n\nclosest_hit continues searching and updates ray's t_max to find the nearest intersection\nany_hit exits on the first intersection during TLAS traversal (useful for shadow rays)\nBoth always agree on whether a hit occurred (hit vs miss)\nany_hit is typically faster than closest_hit due to early termination","title":"Test 3: Complex Scene"},{"category":"section","location":"bvh_hit_tests_content.html#Performance-Comparison","page":"TLAS Hit Testing: closest_hit vs any_hit","text":"Compare the performance of closest_hit vs any_hit.\n\nfunction render_io(obj)\n    io = IOBuffer()\n    show(io, MIME\"text/plain\"(), obj)\n    printer = BonitoBook.HTMLPrinter(io; root_tag = \"span\")\n    str = sprint(io -> show(io, MIME\"text/html\"(), printer))\n    DOM.pre(HTML(str); style=\"font-size: 10px\")\nend\n\nusing BenchmarkTools\nusing Adapt\n\ntest_ray = Raycore.Ray(o=Point3f(0.1, 0.1, -5), d=Vec3f(0, 0, 1))\nstatic_tlas = Adapt.adapt(KA.CPU(), tlas)\n\n# Benchmark closest_hit\nclosest_time = @benchmark Raycore.closest_hit($static_tlas, $test_ray)\n\n# Benchmark any_hit\nany_time = @benchmark Raycore.any_hit($static_tlas, $test_ray)\n\n\nperf_table = map([\n    (\"closest_hit\", closest_time),\n    (\"any_hit\", any_time),\n]) do (method, time_us)\n    (Method = method, Time_μs = render_io(time_us))\nend\nBonito.Table(perf_table)","title":"Performance Comparison"},{"category":"section","location":"bvh_hit_tests_content.html#Summary","page":"TLAS Hit Testing: closest_hit vs any_hit","text":"This document demonstrated:\n\ntrace_rays - A convenient function for tracing rays against a TLAS and collecting results for visualization\nReturns a RayIntersectionResult bundling rays, hit data, and the TLAS\nAutomatically builds a StaticTLAS for traversal\nMakie visualization recipe - Automatic visualization via plot(result)\nAutomatically renders TLAS geometry, rays, and hit points\nCustomizable colors, transparency, markers, and labels\nWorks with any Makie backend (GLMakie, WGLMakie, CairoMakie)\nclosest_hit correctly identifies the nearest intersection among multiple overlapping primitives\nReturns: (hit_found::Bool, triangle::Triangle, distance::Float32, bary_coords::SVector{3,Float32}, instance_id::UInt32)\nUse sum(bary_coords .* triangle.vertices) to convert to world-space hit point\nany_hit efficiently determines if any intersection exists, exiting early\nReturns: Same format as closest_hit\nCan exit early on first hit found, making it faster for occlusion testing\nBoth functions handle miss cases correctly (returning hit_found=false)\nany_hit is typically faster than closest_hit due to early termination\n\nAll tests passed! ✓","title":"Summary"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu_raytracing_tutorial.html#Ray-Tracing-on-the-GPU","page":"Ray Tracing on the GPU","text":"In this tutorial, we'll take the ray tracer from the previous tutorial and port it to the GPU using KernelAbstractions.jl and a GPU backend of choice (CUDA.jl, AMDGPU.jl, OpenCL.jl, OneApi.jl, or Metal.jl). We'll explore three different kernel implementations, each with different optimization strategies, and benchmark their performance against each other.\n\nBy the end, you'll understand how to write efficient GPU kernels for ray tracing and the tradeoffs between different approaches!","title":"Ray Tracing on the GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu_raytracing_tutorial.html#Setup","page":"Ray Tracing on the GPU","text":"using Raycore, GeometryBasics, LinearAlgebra\nusing Colors, ImageShow\nusing WGLMakie\nusing KernelAbstractions\nusing BenchmarkTools\n\nTo run things on the GPU with KernelAbstractions, you need to choose the correct package for your GPU and set the array type we use from there on.\n\n#using CUDA; GArray = CuArray; # For NVIDIA GPUS\n#using AMDGPU; GArray = ROCArray; # for AMD GPUs\n#using Metal; GArray = MtlArray; # for Apple hardware\n#using oneAPI; GArray = oneArray; # for intel\n# OpenCL with the pocl backend should work for most CPUs and some GPUs, but might not be as fast.\n# using pocl_jll, OpenCL; GArray = CLArray;\nGArray = Array # For the tutorial to run on CI we just use the CPU\n\nReady for GPU! We have:\n\nRaycore for fast ray-triangle intersections\nKernelAbstractions for portable GPU kernels (works with CUDA, AMD, Metal, oneAPI, and OpenCL)\nBenchmarkTools for performance comparison","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu_raytracing_tutorial.html#Part-1:-Scene-Setup-(Same-as-CPU-Tutorial)","page":"Ray Tracing on the GPU","text":"Let's use the exact same scene as the CPU tutorial - the Makie cat with room geometry:\n\n# Load and prepare the cat model\ninclude(\"raytracing-core.jl\")\nbvh, ctx = example_scene()\nmd\"**Scene loaded: $(length(bvh.all_blas_prims)) triangles, $(length(ctx.materials)) materials**\"","title":"Part 1: Scene Setup (Same as CPU Tutorial)"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu_raytracing_tutorial.html#Part-2:-GPU-Kernel-Version-1-Basic-Naive-Approach","page":"Ray Tracing on the GPU","text":"The simplest GPU kernel - one thread per pixel:\n\nimport KernelAbstractions as KA\n\n# Basic kernel: one thread per pixel, straightforward implementation\n@kernel function raytrace_kernel_v1!(\n    img, @Const(bvh), @Const(ctx),\n    camera_pos, focal_length, aspect, sky_color, ::Val{NSamples}\n) where {NSamples}\n    # Get pixel coordinates\n    idx = @index(Global, Linear)\n    height, width = size(img)\n    # Convert linear index to 2D coordinates\n    x = ((idx - 1) % width) + 1\n    y = ((idx - 1) ÷ width) + 1\n    if x <= width && y <= height\n        # Generate camera ray and calculate a simple light model\n        color = Vec3f(0)\n        for i in 1:NSamples\n            color = color .+ sample_light(bvh, ctx, width, height, camera_pos, focal_length, aspect, x, y, sky_color)\n        end\n        @inbounds img[y, x] = to_rgb(color ./ NSamples)\n    end\nend\n\nThe trace_gpu function is a universal launcher that works with any of our kernels. It handles the backend-specific setup automatically using KernelAbstractions.jl:\n\nfunction trace_gpu(kernel, img, bvh, ctx;\n        camera_pos=Point3f(0, -0.9, -2.5), fov=45.0f0,\n        sky_color=RGB{Float32}(0.5f0,0.7f0,1.0f0),\n        samples_per_pixel=4,\n        ndrange=length(img), tilesize=nothing\n    )\n    height, width = size(img)\n    aspect = Float32(width / height)\n    focal_length = 1.0f0 / tan(deg2rad(fov / 2))\n\n    # KernelAbstractions automatically detects the backend (CPU/GPU) from the array type\n    backend = KA.get_backend(img)\n\n    # Create the kernel with or without tilesize (for workgroup configuration)\n    kernel! = isnothing(tilesize) ? kernel(backend) : kernel(backend, tilesize)\n\n    kernel!(img, bvh, ctx, camera_pos, focal_length, aspect, sky_color, Val(samples_per_pixel), ndrange=ndrange)\n\n    # Ensure GPU computation completes before returning\n    KA.synchronize(backend)\n    return img\nend\n\nKey KernelAbstractions.jl concepts:\n\nBackend detection: get_backend(array) automatically determines if we're using CPU, AMD GPU, NVIDIA GPU, etc.\nKernel compilation: kernel(backend) compiles the kernel for the specific backend\nWorkgroup configuration: Optional tilesize parameter controls thread organization\nThread indexing: Inside kernels, use @index(Global, Linear) or @index(Global, Cartesian) to get thread IDs\nSynchronization: synchronize(backend) ensures all GPU work completes before continuing\n\nLet's test kernel v1 on the CPU (yes, they always work with normal Arrays):\n\nimg = fill(RGBf(0, 0, 0), 400, 720)\nbench_kernel_cpu_v1 = @benchmark trace_gpu(raytrace_kernel_v1!, img, bvh, ctx)\nimg\n\nTo run things on the GPU, we simply convert the arrays to the GPU backend array type. to_gpu is a helper in Raycore to convert nested structs correctly for the kernel. It's not doing anything special, besides that struct of arrays need to be converted to device arrays and for pure arrays GPUArray(array) is enough.\n\nusing Raycore: to_gpu\nimg = fill(RGBf(0, 0, 0), 400, 720)\nimg_gpu = GArray(img);\nbvh_gpu = to_gpu(GArray, bvh);\nctx_gpu = to_gpu(GArray, ctx);\nbench_kernel_v1 = @benchmark trace_gpu(raytrace_kernel_v1!, img_gpu, bvh_gpu, ctx_gpu)\n# Bring back to CPU to display image\nArray(img_gpu)\n\nFirst GPU render! This is the simplest approach - one thread per pixel with no optimization.","title":"Part 2: GPU Kernel Version 1 - Basic Naive Approach"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu_raytracing_tutorial.html#Part-3:-Optimized-Kernel-Loop-Unrolling","page":"Ray Tracing on the GPU","text":"Loop overhead is significant on GPUs! Manually unrolling the sampling loop eliminates this overhead:\n\n# Optimized kernel: Unrolled sampling loop\n@kernel function raytrace_kernel_unrolled!(\n        img, @Const(bvh), @Const(ctx),\n        camera_pos, focal_length, aspect, sky_color, ::Val{NSamples}\n    ) where {NSamples}\n    idx = @index(Global, Linear)\n    height, width = size(img)\n    x = ((idx - 1) % width) + 1\n    y = ((idx - 1) ÷ width) + 1\n    if x <= width && y <= height\n        # ntuple with compile-time constant for unrolling\n        samples = ntuple(NSamples) do i\n            sample_light(bvh, ctx, width, height,\n                camera_pos, focal_length, aspect,\n                x, y, sky_color\n            )\n        end\n        color = mean(samples)\n        @inbounds img[y, x] = to_rgb(color)\n    end\nend\n\nbench_kernel_unrolled = @benchmark trace_gpu(raytrace_kernel_unrolled!, img_gpu, bvh_gpu, ctx_gpu)\nArray(img_gpu)\n\nThis eliminates branch overhead from loop conditions\nReduces register pressure\nBetter instruction-level parallelism\n1.39x faster than baseline!","title":"Part 3: Optimized Kernel - Loop Unrolling"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu_raytracing_tutorial.html#Part-4:-Tiled-Kernel-with-Optimized-Tile-Size","page":"Ray Tracing on the GPU","text":"The tile size dramatically affects performance. Let's use the optimal size discovered through benchmarking:\n\n# Tiled kernel with optimized tile size\n@kernel function raytrace_kernel_tiled!(\n    img, bvh, ctx,\n    camera_pos, focal_length, aspect, sky_color, ::Val{NSamples}\n) where {NSamples}\n    # Get tile and local coordinates\n    _tile_xy = @index(Group, Cartesian)\n    _local_xy = @index(Local, Cartesian)\n    _groupsize = @groupsize()\n\n    # Direct tuple unpacking is faster than Vec construction\n    tile_x, tile_y = Tuple(_tile_xy)\n    local_x, local_y = Tuple(_local_xy)\n    group_w, group_h = _groupsize\n\n    # Compute global pixel coordinates\n    x = (tile_x - 1) * group_w + local_x\n    y = (tile_y - 1) * group_h + local_y\n\n    height, width = size(img)\n    if x <= width && y <= height\n        samples = ntuple(NSamples) do i\n            sample_light(bvh, ctx, width, height,\n                camera_pos, focal_length, aspect,\n                x, y, sky_color\n            )\n        end\n        color = mean(samples)\n        @inbounds img[y, x] = to_rgb(color)\n    end\nend\nbench_kernel_tiled_32_16 = @benchmark trace_gpu(\n    $raytrace_kernel_tiled!, $img_gpu, $bvh_gpu, $ctx_gpu;\n    ndrange=size($img_gpu), tilesize=(32,16))\n\n# Benchmark two more important tile sizes for comparison\nbench_kernel_tiled_32_32 = @benchmark trace_gpu(\n    $raytrace_kernel_tiled!, $img_gpu, $bvh_gpu, $ctx_gpu;\n    ndrange=size($img_gpu), tilesize=(32,32))\n\nbench_kernel_tiled_8_8 = @benchmark trace_gpu(\n    $raytrace_kernel_tiled!, $img_gpu, $bvh_gpu, $ctx_gpu;\n    ndrange=size($img_gpu), tilesize=(8,8))\n\n# Use optimal tile size: (32, 16) - discovered through benchmarking!\nArray(img_gpu)\n\nTile size matters! With (32, 16) tiles, this kernel is 1.22x faster than baseline. With poor tile sizes like (8, 8), it can be 2.5x slower!","title":"Part 4: Tiled Kernel with Optimized Tile Size"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu_raytracing_tutorial.html#Part-5:-Wavefront-Path-Tracing","page":"Ray Tracing on the GPU","text":"The wavefront approach reorganizes ray tracing to minimize thread divergence by grouping similar work together. Instead of each thread handling an entire pixel's path, we separate the work into stages. Discussing the exact implementation is outside the scope of this tutorial, so we only include the finished renderer here:\n\ninclude(\"wavefront-renderer.jl\")\n\nLet's benchmark the wavefront renderer on both CPU and GPU:\n\n# CPU benchmark\nrenderer_cpu = WavefrontRenderer(img, bvh, ctx)\nbench_wavefront_cpu = @benchmark render!($renderer_cpu)\n\n# GPU benchmark\nrenderer_gpu = to_gpu(GArray, renderer_cpu)\nbench_wavefront_gpu = @benchmark render!($renderer_gpu)\n\nrenderer_gpu = to_gpu(GArray, WavefrontRenderer(img, bvh, ctx; samples_per_pixel=16))\nrender!(renderer_gpu)\n# Display result\nArray(renderer_gpu.framebuffer)\n\nWavefront benefits:\n\nReduces thread divergence by grouping similar work\nBetter memory access patterns\nScales well with scene complexity\nEnables advanced features like path tracing","title":"Part 5: Wavefront Path Tracing"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu_raytracing_tutorial.html#Part-6:-Comprehensive-Performance-Benchmarks","page":"Ray Tracing on the GPU","text":"Now let's compare all kernels including the wavefront renderer:\n\nbenchmarks = [\n    bench_kernel_v1, bench_kernel_cpu_v1, bench_kernel_unrolled,\n    bench_kernel_tiled_8_8, bench_kernel_tiled_32_16, bench_kernel_tiled_32_32,\n    bench_wavefront_cpu, bench_wavefront_gpu\n]\nlabels = [\n    \"Baseline\\n(gpu)\", \"Baseline\\n(cpu)\", \"Unrolled\",\n    \"Tiled\\n(8×8)\", \"Tiled\\n(32×16)\", \"Tiled\\n(32×32)\",\n    \"Wavefront\\n(cpu)\", \"Wavefront\\n(gpu)\"\n]\n\nfig, times, speedups = plot_kernel_benchmarks(benchmarks, labels)\n# save(data\"gpu-benchmarks.png\", fig; backend=Main.GLMakie)\n# We use the Fig from my local run, since on CI this won't show the differences\nDOM.img(src=Asset(data\"gpu-benchmarks.png\"), width=\"700px\")","title":"Part 6: Comprehensive Performance Benchmarks"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu_raytracing_tutorial.html#Next-Steps","page":"Ray Tracing on the GPU","text":"Add adaptive sampling (more samples only where needed)\nExplore shared memory optimizations for BVH traversal\nImplement streaming multisampling across frames","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"raytracing_tutorial_content.html#Ray-Tracing-in-one-Hour","page":"Ray Tracing in one Hour","text":"Analogous to the famous Ray Tracing in one Weekend, this tutorial uses Raycore to do the hard work of performant ray triangle intersection and therefore get a high performing ray tracer in a much shorter time. We'll start with the absolute basics and progressively add features until we have a ray tracer that produces beautiful images with shadows, materials, and reflections.","title":"Ray Tracing in one Hour"},{"category":"section","location":"raytracing_tutorial_content.html#Setup","page":"Ray Tracing in one Hour","text":"using Raycore, GeometryBasics, LinearAlgebra\nusing Colors, ImageShow, WGLMakie\nusing MeshIO\nusing BenchmarkTools\n\nReady to go! We have:\n\nRaycore for fast ray-triangle intersections\nGeometryBasics for geometry primitives\nColors and ImageShow for displaying rendered images\nMeshIO for loading the cat data","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"raytracing_tutorial_content.html#Part-1:-Our-Scene,-The-Makie-Cat","page":"Ray Tracing in one Hour","text":"Let's create a fun scene that we'll use throughout this tutorial.\n\n# Load the cat model and rotate it to face the camera\ncat_mesh = Makie.loadasset(\"cat.obj\")\nangle = deg2rad(150f0)\nrotation = Makie.Quaternionf(0, sin(angle/2), 0, cos(angle/2))\nrotated_coords = [rotation * Point3f(v) for v in coordinates(cat_mesh)]\n\n# Get bounding box and translate cat to sit on the floor\ncat_bbox = Rect3f(rotated_coords)\nfloor_y = -1.5f0\ncat_offset = Vec3f(0, floor_y - cat_bbox.origin[2], 0)\n\ncat_mesh = GeometryBasics.normal_mesh(\n    [v + cat_offset for v in rotated_coords],\n    faces(cat_mesh)\n)\n\n# Create a simple room: floor, back wall, and side wall\nfloor = normal_mesh(Rect3f(Vec3f(-5, -1.5, -2), Vec3f(10, 0.01, 10)))\nback_wall = normal_mesh(Rect3f(Vec3f(-5, -1.5, 8), Vec3f(10, 5, 0.01)))\nleft_wall = normal_mesh(Rect3f(Vec3f(-5, -1.5, -2), Vec3f(0.01, 5, 10)))\n\n# Add a couple of spheres for visual interest\nsphere1 = Tesselation(Sphere(Point3f(-2, -1.5 + 0.8, 2), 0.8f0), 64)\nsphere2 = Tesselation(Sphere(Point3f(2, -1.5 + 0.6, 1), 0.6f0), 64)\n\n# Build our TLAS acceleration structure\nscene_geometry = [cat_mesh, floor, back_wall, left_wall,\n    normal_mesh(sphere1), normal_mesh(sphere2)]\nbvh = Raycore.TLAS(scene_geometry, (mesh_idx, tri_idx) -> UInt32(mesh_idx))\nmd\"**TLAS built**\"","title":"Part 1: Our Scene, The Makie Cat"},{"category":"section","location":"raytracing_tutorial_content.html#Part-2:-Helper-Functions-Building-Blocks","page":"Ray Tracing in one Hour","text":"Let's define reusable helper functions we'll use throughout:\n\n# Compute interpolated normal at hit point\nfunction compute_normal(triangle, bary_coords)\n    v0, v1, v2 = Raycore.normals(triangle)\n    u, v, w = bary_coords[1], bary_coords[2], bary_coords[3]\n    return Vec3f(normalize(v0 * u + v1 * v + v2 * w))\nend\n\n# Generate camera ray for a pixel with optional jitter\nfunction camera_ray(x, y, width, height, camera_pos, focal_length, aspect; jitter=Vec2f(0))\n    ndc_x = (2.0f0 * (Float32(x) - 0.5f0 + jitter[1]) / Float32(width) - 1.0f0) * aspect\n    ndc_y = 1.0f0 - 2.0f0 * (Float32(y) - 0.5f0 + jitter[2]) / Float32(height)\n    direction = normalize(Vec3f(ndc_x, ndc_y, focal_length))\n    return Raycore.Ray(o=camera_pos, d=direction)\nend\n\n# Convert between color representations\nto_vec3f(c::RGB) = Vec3f(c.r, c.g, c.b)\nto_rgb(v::Vec3f) = RGB{Float32}(v...)","title":"Part 2: Helper Functions - Building Blocks"},{"category":"section","location":"raytracing_tutorial_content.html#Part-3:-The-Simplest-Ray-Tracer-Depth-Visualization","page":"Ray Tracing in one Hour","text":"We're using one main function to shoot rays for each pixel. For simplicity, we already added multisampling and simple multi threading, to enjoy smoother images and faster rendering times throughout the tutorial. Read the GPU tutorial how to further improve the performance of this simple, not yet optimal kernel.\n\nfunction trace(f, bvh; width=700, height=300,\n               camera_pos=Point3f(0, -0.9, -2.5), fov=45.0f0,\n               sky_color=RGB{Float32}(0.5f0, 0.7f0, 1.0f0),\n               samples=1, ctx=nothing)\n    img = Matrix{RGB{Float32}}(undef, height, width)\n    aspect = Float32(width / height)\n    focal_length = 1.0f0 / tan(deg2rad(fov / 2))\n\n    Threads.@threads for y in 1:height\n        for x in 1:width\n            color_sum = Vec3f(0)\n            for _ in 1:samples\n                jitter = samples > 1 ? rand(Vec2f) : Vec2f(0)\n                # Calculate the ray shooting from the camera pixel into the scene\n                ray = camera_ray(x, y, width, height, camera_pos, focal_length, aspect; jitter)\n                hit_found, triangle, distance, bary_coords, _ = Raycore.closest_hit(bvh, ray)\n                color = if hit_found\n                    to_vec3f(f(bvh, ctx, triangle, distance, bary_coords, ray))\n                else\n                    to_vec3f(sky_color)\n                end\n                color_sum += color\n            end\n\n            img[y, x] = to_rgb(color_sum / samples)\n        end\n    end\n    return img\nend\n\n# Visualize depth\ndepth_kernel(bvh, ctx, tri, dist, bary, ray) = RGB(1.0f0 - min(dist / 10.0f0, 1.0f0))\n\n@time trace(depth_kernel, bvh, samples=16)\n\nFirst render! Depth visualization shows distance to surfaces. Much faster with threading and smoother with multi-sampling!","title":"Part 3: The Simplest Ray Tracer - Depth Visualization"},{"category":"section","location":"raytracing_tutorial_content.html#Part-5:-Lighting-with-Hard-Shadows","page":"Ray Tracing in one Hour","text":"Let's add lighting and shadows using a reusable lighting function:\n\n# Reusable lighting function with optional shadow sampling\nfunction compute_light(\n        bvh, point, normal, light_pos, light_intensity, light_color; shadow_samples=1)\n\n    light_vec = light_pos - point\n    light_dist = norm(light_vec)\n    light_dir = light_vec / light_dist\n\n    diffuse = max(0.0f0, dot(normal, light_dir))\n\n    # Shadow testing with optional soft shadows\n    shadow_factor = 0.0f0\n    light_radius = 0.2f0  # Size of area light for soft shadows\n\n    for _ in 1:shadow_samples\n        # For shadow_samples=1, this is just the light position (hard shadow)\n        # For shadow_samples>1, we sample random points on a disk (soft shadow)\n        if shadow_samples > 1\n            # Random point on disk perpendicular to light direction\n            offset = (rand(Vec3f) .* 2.0f0 .- 1.0f0) * light_radius\n            offset = offset - light_dir * dot(offset, light_dir)\n            shadow_target = light_pos + offset\n        else\n            shadow_target = light_pos\n        end\n\n        shadow_vec = shadow_target - point\n        shadow_dist = norm(shadow_vec)\n        shadow_dir = normalize(shadow_vec)\n\n        shadow_ray = Raycore.Ray(o=point + normal * 0.001f0, d=shadow_dir)\n        shadow_hit, _, hit_dist, _, _ = Raycore.any_hit(bvh, shadow_ray)\n\n        if !shadow_hit || hit_dist >= shadow_dist\n            shadow_factor += 1.0f0\n        end\n    end\n    shadow_factor /= shadow_samples\n\n    # Compute final light contribution\n    attenuation = light_intensity / (light_dist * light_dist)\n    return to_vec3f(light_color) * (diffuse * attenuation * shadow_factor)\nend\n\nfunction shadow_kernel(bvh, ctx, tri, dist, bary, ray; shadow_samples=1)\n    hit_point = ray.o + ray.d * dist\n    normal = compute_normal(tri, bary)\n    # Single point light\n    light_pos = Point3f(3, 4, -2)\n    light_intensity = 50.0f0\n    light_color = RGB{Float32}(1.0f0, 0.9f0, 0.8f0)\n    # Hard shadows (shadow_samples=1)\n    light_contrib = compute_light(\n        bvh, hit_point, normal, light_pos, light_intensity, light_color;\n        shadow_samples=shadow_samples\n    )\n    ambient = 0.1f0\n\n    brightness = ambient .+ light_contrib\n    return to_rgb(brightness)\nend\n\ntrace(shadow_kernel, bvh, samples=4)\n\nHard shadows working! Scene has realistic lighting with sharp shadow edges.","title":"Part 5: Lighting with Hard Shadows"},{"category":"section","location":"raytracing_tutorial_content.html#Part-6:-Soft-Shadows","page":"Ray Tracing in one Hour","text":"Now let's make shadows more realistic by sampling the light as an area light:\n\ntrace((args...)-> shadow_kernel(args...; shadow_samples=8), bvh, samples=8)\n\nSoft shadows! Much more realistic with smooth penumbra edges.","title":"Part 6: Soft Shadows"},{"category":"section","location":"raytracing_tutorial_content.html#Part-7:-Materials-and-Multiple-Lights","page":"Ray Tracing in one Hour","text":"Time to add color and multiple lights! To associate materials with geometry, we need to rebuild the TLAS with metadata that links each triangle to its material.","title":"Part 7: Materials and Multiple Lights"},{"category":"section","location":"raytracing_tutorial_content.html#Triangle-Metadata","page":"Ray Tracing in one Hour","text":"When building a TLAS, you can pass a metadata_fn(mesh_idx, tri_idx) that assigns custom data to each triangle. This metadata is stored in triangle.metadata and returned with every ray hit. For materials, we use the mesh index as metadata:\n\nstruct PointLight\n    position::Point3f\n    intensity::Float32\n    color::RGB{Float32}\nend\n\nstruct Material\n    base_color::RGB{Float32}\n    metallic::Float32\n    roughness::Float32\nend\n\nstruct RenderContext\n    lights::Vector{PointLight}\n    materials::Vector{Material}\n    ambient::Float32\nend\n\n# Define materials for each mesh (order matches scene_geometry)\nmaterials = [\n    Material(RGB(0.8f0, 0.6f0, 0.4f0), 0.0f0, 0.8f0),  # 1: cat\n    Material(RGB(0.3f0, 0.5f0, 0.3f0), 0.0f0, 0.9f0),  # 2: floor\n    Material(RGB(0.8f0, 0.6f0, 0.5f0), 0.8f0, 0.05f0), # 3: back wall\n    Material(RGB(0.7f0, 0.7f0, 0.8f0), 0.0f0, 0.8f0),  # 4: left wall\n    Material(RGB(0.9f0, 0.9f0, 0.9f0), 0.8f0, 0.02f0), # 5: sphere1 - metallic\n    Material(RGB(0.3f0, 0.6f0, 0.9f0), 0.5f0, 0.3f0),  # 6: sphere2 - semi-metallic\n]\n\n# Rebuild TLAS with material indices as metadata\n# The metadata_fn receives (mesh_idx, tri_idx) and returns data stored per-triangle\nbvh = Raycore.TLAS(scene_geometry, (mesh_idx, tri_idx) -> UInt32(mesh_idx))\n\n# Create lights\nlights = [\n    PointLight(Point3f(3, 4, -2), 50.0f0, RGB(1.0f0, 0.9f0, 0.8f0)),\n    PointLight(Point3f(-3, 2, 0), 20.0f0, RGB(0.7f0, 0.8f0, 1.0f0)),\n    PointLight(Point3f(0, 5, 5), 15.0f0, RGB(1.0f0, 1.0f0, 1.0f0))\n]\n\nctx = RenderContext(lights, materials, 0.1f0)\nnothing\n\nNow when a ray hits a triangle, we can look up its material using triangle.metadata:\n\nmat = ctx.materials[triangle.metadata]  # Get material for hit triangle\n\n# Compute lighting from all lights - reusing our compute_light function!\nfunction compute_multi_light(bvh, ctx, point, normal, mat; shadow_samples=1)\n    base_color = to_vec3f(mat.base_color)\n    total_color = base_color * ctx.ambient\n\n    for light in ctx.lights\n        light_contrib = compute_light(bvh, point, normal, light.position, light.intensity, light.color, shadow_samples=shadow_samples)\n        total_color += base_color .* light_contrib\n    end\n\n    return total_color\nend\n\nfunction material_kernel(bvh, ctx, tri, dist, bary, ray)\n    hit_point = ray.o + ray.d * dist\n    normal = compute_normal(tri, bary)\n    # Look up material using triangle's metadata (mesh index we stored during BVH construction)\n    mat = ctx.materials[tri.metadata]\n\n    color = compute_multi_light(bvh, ctx, hit_point, normal, mat, shadow_samples=2)\n    return to_rgb(color)\nend\n\ntrace(material_kernel, bvh, samples=4, ctx=ctx)\n\nColorful scene with soft shadows from multiple lights! Each object has its own material.","title":"Triangle Metadata"},{"category":"section","location":"raytracing_tutorial_content.html#Part-8:-Reflections","page":"Ray Tracing in one Hour","text":"Add simple reflections for metallic surfaces:\n\nfunction reflective_kernel(bvh, ctx, tri, dist, bary, ray, sky_color)\n    hit_point = ray.o + ray.d * dist\n    normal = compute_normal(tri, bary)\n    mat = ctx.materials[tri.metadata]\n\n    # Direct lighting with soft shadows\n    direct_color = compute_multi_light(bvh, ctx, hit_point, normal, mat, shadow_samples=8)\n\n    # Reflections for metallic surfaces\n    if mat.metallic > 0.0f0\n        wo = -ray.d\n        reflect_dir = Raycore.reflect(wo, normal)\n\n        # Optional roughness\n        if mat.roughness > 0.0f0\n            offset = (rand(Vec3f) .* 2.0f0 .- 1.0f0) * mat.roughness\n            reflect_dir = normalize(reflect_dir + offset)\n        end\n\n        # Cast reflection ray\n        reflect_ray = Raycore.Ray(o=hit_point + normal * 0.001f0, d=reflect_dir)\n        refl_hit, refl_tri, refl_dist, refl_bary, _ = Raycore.closest_hit(bvh, reflect_ray)\n\n        reflection_color = if refl_hit\n            refl_point = reflect_ray.o + reflect_ray.d * refl_dist\n            refl_normal = compute_normal(refl_tri, refl_bary)\n            refl_mat = ctx.materials[refl_tri.metadata]\n            compute_multi_light(bvh, ctx, refl_point, refl_normal, refl_mat, shadow_samples=1)\n        else\n            to_vec3f(sky_color)\n        end\n\n        direct_color = direct_color * (1.0f0 - mat.metallic) + reflection_color * mat.metallic\n    end\n\n    return to_rgb(direct_color)\nend\n\nimg = trace(bvh, samples=16, ctx=ctx) do bvh, ctx, tri, dist, bary, ray\n    reflective_kernel(bvh, ctx, tri, dist, bary, ray, RGB(0.5f0, 0.7f0, 1.0f0))\nend\n\n# Tone mapping functions\nluminosity(c::RGB{T}) where {T} = (max(c.r, c.g, c.b) + min(c.r, c.g, c.b)) / 2.0f0\n\nfunction avg_lum(rgb_m, δ::Number=1f-10)\n    cumsum = 0.0f0\n    for pix in rgb_m\n        cumsum += log10(δ + luminosity(pix))\n    end\n    return 10^(cumsum / (prod(size(rgb_m))))\nend\n\nfunction tone_mapping(img; a=0.5f0, y=1.0f0, lum=avg_lum(img, 1f-10))\n    img_normalized = img .* a .* (1.0f0 / lum)\n    img_01 = map(col->mapc(c-> clamp(c, 0f0, 1f0), col), img_normalized)\n    ycorrected = map(col->mapc(c-> c^(1f0 / y), col), img_01)\n    return ycorrected\nend\n\ntone_mapping(img, a=0.38, y=1.0)\n\nFor performance type stability is a must! We can use JET to test if a function is completely type stable, which we also test in the Raycore tests for all functions.\n\nusing JET\n\n# Get test data\ntest_ray = camera_ray(350, 150, 700, 300, Point3f(0, -0.9, -2.5), 1.0f0 / tan(deg2rad(45.0f0 / 2)), Float32(700/300))\nhit_found, test_tri, test_dist, test_bary = Raycore.closest_hit(bvh, test_ray)\n\n# Check kernel type stability (filter to Main module to ignore Base internals)\n@test_opt depth_kernel(bvh, ctx, test_tri, test_dist, test_bary, test_ray)\n@test_opt shadow_kernel(bvh, ctx, test_tri, test_dist, test_bary, test_ray)\n@test_opt material_kernel(bvh, ctx, test_tri, test_dist, test_bary, test_ray)\n@test_opt reflective_kernel(bvh, ctx, test_tri, test_dist, test_bary, test_ray, RGB(0.5f0, 0.7f0, 1.0f0))\nnothing","title":"Part 8: Reflections"},{"category":"section","location":"raytracing_tutorial_content.html#Summary","page":"Ray Tracing in one Hour","text":"We built a complete ray tracer with:\n\nCore Features:\n\nTLAS acceleration for fast ray-scene intersections\nPerspective camera with configurable FOV\nSmooth shading from interpolated normals\nMulti-light system with distance attenuation\nSoft shadows using area light sampling (via compute_light with shadow_samples)\nMaterial system (base color, metallic, roughness)\nReflections with optional roughness\nACES tone mapping for HDR\n\nPerformance:\n\nMulti-threading for parallel rendering (introduced early!)\nMulti-sampling for anti-aliasing (introduced early!)\nType-stable kernels for optimal performance\nModular, reusable compute_light function - works for both hard and soft shadows\n\nKey Raycore Functions:\n\nRaycore.TLAS(meshes) - Build acceleration structure\nRaycore.TLAS(meshes, metadata_fn) - Build with custom per-triangle metadata\nRaycore.Ray(o=origin, d=direction) - Create ray\nRaycore.closest_hit(tlas, ray) - Find nearest intersection, returns (hit, triangle, distance, bary_coords, instance_id)\nRaycore.any_hit(tlas, ray) - Test for any intersection (fast shadow test)\nRaycore.reflect(wo, normal) - Compute reflection direction\ntriangle.metadata - Access custom data stored per-triangle\n\nKey Patterns:\n\nMaterial Scene Pattern - Associate materials with geometry using metadata:\n\n# Build TLAS with mesh index as metadata\ntlas = Raycore.TLAS(meshes, (mesh_idx, tri_idx) -> UInt32(mesh_idx))\n\n# In your shader, look up material from hit triangle\nmat = materials[triangle.metadata]\n\nReusable Lighting - The compute_light function handles both hard and soft shadows:\n\nshadow_samples=1 → hard shadows\nshadow_samples>1 → soft shadows\n\nThis shows how well-designed functions can handle multiple use cases cleanly!","title":"Summary"},{"category":"section","location":"hw_acceleration.html#Hardware-Ray-Tracing-with-Mantle","page":"Hardware RT Acceleration","text":"Modern GPUs traverse BVHs and intersect triangles in fixed-function hardware (RT cores on NVIDIA, Ray Accelerators on AMD, the ray-tracing units of Apple's M3 and later). Raycore's API is written so a kernel does not care which kind of acceleration structure it traces: Mantle implements it for the hardware structures, on Vulkan (through the Lava compiler, as a ray query) and on Metal (through its intersector).\n\nThe demo below builds the same scene twice, into a software Raycore.TLAS and into a hardware Mantle.HWTLAS, traces one camera ray per pixel through both with the same kernel, and checks that the depth buffers agree.","title":"Hardware Ray Tracing with Mantle"},{"category":"section","location":"hw_acceleration.html#When-to-pick-Raycore.TLAS-vs.-Mantle.HWTLAS","page":"Hardware RT Acceleration","text":"Aspect Raycore.TLAS Mantle.HWTLAS\nBackend any KernelAbstractions backend Mantle on Vulkan with ray query, or on Metal with ray tracing\nBVH software (LBVH over instanced BLASes) VkAccelerationStructureKHR / MTLAccelerationStructure\nTracing code a KA @kernel calling Raycore.closest_hit(accel, ray) the same kernel\nUse when portability, no RT hardware, CPU backends RT hardware\n\nBoth satisfy Raycore.AbstractAccel: push!, delete!, update_transform! (one transform for every instance of a handle), update_transforms!, set_visible!, sync!, n_instances, n_geometries, world_bound and wait_for_gpu! mean the same on both. So do the per-instance cull masks (push!(…; instance_mask), traced with closest_hit(accel, ray, mask)), the instance records a kernel may write (instance_buffer(accel, handle), then Raycore.refit!(accel)), and the hit itself: closest_hit and any_hit return the triangle in world space, the distance t, and as their fifth value the instance's custom index (instance_id at push!, low 24 bits), on both.","title":"When to pick Raycore.TLAS vs. Mantle.HWTLAS"},{"category":"section","location":"hw_acceleration.html#When-hardware-RT-helps","page":"Hardware RT Acceleration","text":"The biggest gains come from scenes with many triangles, deep occlusion (many traversal steps per ray) and cheap shading. For a trivial scene the software BVH already runs at memory bandwidth; the ~48k triangles below are enough to see the hardware pull ahead and small enough for a tutorial.\n\nMantle.supports_hwtlas(backend) says whether a device has the hardware path.","title":"When hardware RT helps"},{"category":"section","location":"hw_acceleration.html#Setup","page":"Hardware RT Acceleration","text":"using Raycore, Mantle, GeometryBasics, LinearAlgebra, Adapt\nimport KernelAbstractions as KA\nusing KernelAbstractions: @kernel, @index, @Const\n\nbackend = Mantle.defaultbackend()     # the platform's device: Vulkan, or Metal on a Mac\nMantle.supports_hwtlas(backend)       # true on an RT-capable device","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"hw_acceleration.html#Building-the-scene-twice","page":"Hardware RT Acceleration","text":"Tessellated spheres on a floor. Both structures take plain GeometryBasics.Mesh objects through push!.\n\nfunction build_meshes()\n    floor = GeometryBasics.normal_mesh(Rect3f(Vec3f(-3, -3, -0.01), Vec3f(6, 6, 0.01)))\n    centers = [Point3f(i * 0.9f0, j * 0.9f0, 0.4f0) for i in -2:2 for j in -2:2]\n    spheres = [GeometryBasics.normal_mesh(Tesselation(Sphere(c, 0.3f0), 32)) for c in centers]\n    return [floor; spheres]\nend\n\nmeshes = build_meshes()\nsw = Raycore.TLAS(backend)\nhw = Mantle.HWTLAS{Raycore.Triangle{UInt32}}(backend)\nfor (i, m) in enumerate(meshes)\n    push!(sw, m; instance_id = UInt32(i))\n    push!(hw, m; instance_id = UInt32(i))\nend\nRaycore.sync!(sw)\nRaycore.sync!(hw)\n\n26 meshes, 26 instances in each, 48 062 triangles. sync! uploads the meshes and builds the structures: GPU LBVH builds (one BLAS per mesh and a TLAS over the instances) for Raycore.TLAS, the driver's acceleration-structure builds for Mantle.HWTLAS.","title":"Building the scene twice"},{"category":"section","location":"hw_acceleration.html#One-kernel,-both-structures","page":"Hardware RT Acceleration","text":"const W, H = 256, 192\ncam = Point3f(0, -3.5, 1.6)\nforward = normalize(Point3f(0, 0, 0.3) - cam)\nright = normalize(cross(forward, Vec3f(0, 0, 1)))\nup = cross(right, forward)\nfocal = 1f0 / tan(deg2rad(45f0 / 2))\nrays = [Raycore.Ray(o = cam, d = Vec3f(normalize(forward * focal +\n                                                 right * ((2f0 * (x - 0.5f0) / W - 1f0) * Float32(W / H)) +\n                                                 up * (1f0 - 2f0 * (y - 0.5f0) / H))))\n        for x in 1:W, y in 1:H]\n\n@kernel function depth!(depth, @Const(rays), accel)\n    i = @index(Global, Linear)\n    @inbounds begin\n        hit, _, t, _, _ = Raycore.closest_hit(accel, rays[i])\n        depth[i] = hit ? t : -1f0\n    end\nend\n\nrays_dev = Adapt.adapt(backend, vec(rays))\ndepth_sw = KA.zeros(backend, Float32, W * H)\ndepth_hw = KA.zeros(backend, Float32, W * H)\nk = depth!(backend, 64)\ntrace!(depth, accel) = (k(depth, rays_dev, Adapt.adapt(backend, accel); ndrange = W * H); KA.synchronize(backend))\ntrace!(depth_sw, sw)\ntrace!(depth_hw, hw)\n\nAdapt.adapt(backend, accel) is what a kernel receives: a StaticTLAS for the software structure, an AdaptedAccel for the hardware one. Call it per dispatch — after a mutation and sync! it may be a different object — and never cache it across mutations.\n\nMeasured on a Radeon 8060S (RADV) and on an Apple M5:\n\n Radeon 8060S Apple M5\nhit-mask disagreement 0 of 49 152 0 of 49 152\nmax depth difference 1.3e-5 1.8e-5\nsoftware, one frame 0.22 ms 1.0 ms\nhardware, one frame 0.10 ms 0.47 ms\n\nThe remaining depth difference is rounding: both intersect the same triangles, the hardware in its own arithmetic. The timing ratio depends on triangle count, ray coherence and the GPU; measure your scene.","title":"One kernel, both structures"},{"category":"section","location":"hw_acceleration.html#Lifetime","page":"Hardware RT Acceleration","text":"sync! owns the adapted form: consumers re-read it, through Adapt.adapt, per dispatch. sync! does not block the CPU; buffers an old structure used stay alive until the GPU is past every submission that read them. For a CPU-side drain, before tear-down or between benchmark phases, call Raycore.wait_for_gpu!(accel).","title":"Lifetime"},{"category":"section","location":"hw_acceleration.html#Ray-tracing-pipelines-(Vulkan-only)","page":"Hardware RT Acceleration","text":"Beyond ray queries from compute kernels, Mantle can run a Vulkan ray-tracing pipeline — raygen, closest-hit, any-hit and miss shaders written in Julia and compiled by Lava, which exposes the lava_rt_* intrinsics for them — through Mantle.RayTracingPipeline and Mantle.trace_rays!. Metal has no counterpart reachable from Julia kernels, so code meant to run on both should trace from a kernel as above.","title":"Ray-tracing pipelines (Vulkan only)"},{"category":"section","location":"viewfactors.html#View-Factors-and-More","page":"View Factors and More","text":"App(Bonito.var\"#27#28\"{Main.var\"#2#3\"}(Main.var\"#2#3\"()), Base.RefValue{Union{Nothing, Session}}(nothing), \"Bonito App\", nothing)","title":"View Factors and More"},{"category":"section","location":"viewfactors_content.html#View-Factors-Analysis","page":"View Factors Analysis","text":"This example demonstrates Raycore's analysis capabilities for radiosity and illumination calculations.","title":"View Factors Analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"viewfactors_content.html#Scene-Setup","page":"View Factors Analysis","text":"using Raycore, GeometryBasics, LinearAlgebra\nusing WGLMakie\nimport KernelAbstractions as KA\n\nfunction LowSphere(radius, contact=Point3f(0); ntriangles=6)\n    return Tesselation(Sphere(contact .+ Point3f(0, 0, radius), radius), ntriangles)\nend\n\n# Create scene with multiple objects (using low triangle counts to keep docs small)\nntriangles = 6\ns1 = LowSphere(0.5f0, Point3f(-0.5, 0.0, 0); ntriangles)\ns2 = LowSphere(0.3f0, Point3f(1, 0.5, 0); ntriangles)\ns3 = LowSphere(0.3f0, Point3f(-0.5, 1, 0); ntriangles)\ns4 = LowSphere(0.4f0, Point3f(0, 1.0, 0); ntriangles)\ns5 = LowSphere(0.35f0, Point3f(0.5, 0.0, 0); ntriangles)\n\n# Build meshes and TLAS with sequential triangle indices as metadata\nsphere_meshes = [normal_mesh(s) for s in [s1, s2, s3, s4, s5]]\ntlas = Raycore.TLAS(sphere_meshes, (mesh_idx, tri_idx) -> begin\n    offset = sum(length(faces(sphere_meshes[i])) for i in 1:mesh_idx-1; init=0)\n    UInt32(offset + tri_idx)\nend)\n\n# Build visualization mesh from TLAS primitives (which may have degenerate triangles removed)\n# This guarantees face count matches between the TLAS and the visualization mesh.\nfunction tlas_to_mesh(tlas)\n    verts = Point3f[]\n    norms = Vec3f[]\n    fs = GLTriangleFace[]\n    for tri in tlas.all_blas_prims\n        base = length(verts)\n        for v in tri.vertices\n            push!(verts, v)\n        end\n        for n in tri.normals\n            push!(norms, Vec3f(n))\n        end\n        push!(fs, GLTriangleFace(base + 1, base + 2, base + 3))\n    end\n    # GeometryBasics ≥ 0.5 dropped `meta(...)`; vertex normals go via the\n    # `normal` kwarg on `GeometryBasics.mesh`/`Mesh`.\n    GeometryBasics.mesh(verts, fs; normal=norms)\nend\nworld_mesh = tlas_to_mesh(tlas)\nN = length(tlas.all_blas_prims)\n\n# Visualize the scene\nf, ax, pl = mesh(world_mesh, color=:teal, axis=(show_axis=false,))\ncenter!(ax.scene)\nf","title":"Scene Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"viewfactors_content.html#View-Factors","page":"View Factors Analysis","text":"View factors quantify how much each surface \"sees\" every other surface - essential for radiosity calculations.\n\n# Calculate view factors between all faces\nviewf_matrix = view_factors(tlas, rays_per_triangle=20)\n\n# Sum up total view factor per face\nviewfacts = map(i -> Float32(sum(view(viewf_matrix, :, i))), 1:N)\n\n# Visualize\nper_face_vf = FaceView(viewfacts, [GLTriangleFace(i) for i in 1:N])\nviewfact_mesh = GeometryBasics.mesh(world_mesh, color=per_face_vf)\nmesh(viewfact_mesh, colormap=:turbo, shading=false,\n     lowclip=:black, colorrange=(0f0, maximum(viewfacts)),\n     axis=(show_axis=false,))\n\nHigher values (warm colors) indicate faces that see more of the surrounding geometry.","title":"View Factors"},{"category":"section","location":"viewfactors_content.html#Illumination","page":"View Factors Analysis","text":"Calculate how much each face is exposed to rays from a specific viewing direction.\n\n# Get camera view direction\nviewdir = normalize(ax.scene.camera.view_direction[])\n\n# Compute illumination\nillum = get_illumination(tlas, viewdir; grid_size=10)\n\n# Visualize\npf = FaceView(illum, [GLTriangleFace(i) for i in 1:N])\nillum_mesh = GeometryBasics.mesh(world_mesh, color=pf)\nmesh(illum_mesh, colormap=[:black, :yellow], colorscale=sqrt,\n     shading=false, axis=(show_axis=false,))\n\nFaces directly visible from the viewing direction show higher illumination (yellow).","title":"Illumination"},{"category":"section","location":"viewfactors_content.html#Centroid-Calculation","page":"View Factors Analysis","text":"Find the average position of visible surface points from a given direction.\n\n# Calculate centroid\nhitpoints, centroid = get_centroid(tlas, viewdir; grid_size=10)\n\n# Visualize\nf, ax, pl = mesh(world_mesh, color=(:blue, 0.5), transparency=true, axis=(show_axis=false,))\neyepos = ax.scene.camera.eyeposition[]\ndepth = map(x -> norm(x .- eyepos), hitpoints)\nmeshscatter!(ax, hitpoints, color=depth, colormap=[:gray, :black], markersize=0.01)\nmeshscatter!(ax, [centroid], color=:red, markersize=0.05)\nf\n\nThe red sphere marks the centroid - useful for camera placement and focus calculations.","title":"Centroid Calculation"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu_raytracing.html#GPU-Ray-Tracing-with-Raycore","page":"GPU Ray Tracing Tutorial","text":"App(Bonito.var\"#27#28\"{Main.var\"#2#3\"}(Main.var\"#2#3\"()), Base.RefValue{Union{Nothing, Session}}(nothing), \"Bonito App\", nothing)","title":"GPU Ray Tracing with Raycore"},{"category":"section","location":"bvh_hit_tests.html#BVH-Hit-tests","page":"BVH Hit Tests","text":"App(Bonito.var\"#27#28\"{Main.var\"#2#3\"}(Main.var\"#2#3\"()), Base.RefValue{Union{Nothing, Session}}(nothing), \"Bonito App\", nothing)","title":"BVH Hit tests"},{"category":"section","location":"raytracing_tutorial.html#Ray-Tracing-with-Raycore","page":"Ray Tracing Tutorial","text":"App(Bonito.var\"#27#28\"{Main.var\"#2#3\"}(Main.var\"#2#3\"()), Base.RefValue{Union{Nothing, Session}}(nothing), \"Bonito App\", nothing)","title":"Ray Tracing with Raycore"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Raycore.jl","page":"Home","text":"High-performance ray-triangle intersection engine with TLAS/BLAS acceleration for CPU and GPU.","title":"Raycore.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Features","page":"Home","text":"Fast TLAS/BLAS acceleration for ray-triangle intersection\nCPU and GPU support via KernelAbstractions.jl\nMultiTypeSet: GPU-safe heterogeneous collections with compile-time type-stable dispatch for materials, textures, lights, etc.\nGPU TLAS: Raycore.TLAS is software, backend-agnostic (any KA backend). For hardware ray tracing on Vulkan and Metal, use Mantle.HWTLAS, a drop-in AbstractAccel. See Hardware Ray Tracing with Mantle.\nAnalysis tools: centroid calculation, illumination analysis, view factors for radiosity\nMakie integration for visualization","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Interactive-Examples","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Interactive Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Hit-Tests-and-Basics","page":"Home","text":"Learn the basics of ray-triangle intersection, TLAS construction, and visualization.\n\n(Image: Basics)\n\nBVH Hit tests","title":"Hit Tests & Basics"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Ray-Tracing-Tutorial","page":"Home","text":"Build a complete ray tracer from scratch with shadows, materials, reflections, and tone mapping.\n\n(Image: Ray Tracing)\n\nRay Tracing with Raycore","title":"Ray Tracing Tutorial"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#GPU-Ray-Tracing-Tutorial","page":"Home","text":"Port the ray tracer to the GPU with KernelAbstractions.jl. Learn about kernel optimization, loop unrolling, tiling, and wavefront rendering.\n\n(Image: GPU Ray Tracing)\n\nGPU Ray Tracing with Raycore","title":"GPU Ray Tracing Tutorial"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Hardware-RT-Acceleration","page":"Home","text":"Use dedicated GPU ray tracing hardware (RT cores / Ray Accelerators) for transparent BVH acceleration via Vulkan and Metal.\n\n(Image: Hardware RT)\n\nHardware Ray Tracing with Mantle","title":"Hardware RT Acceleration"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#View-Factors-Analysis","page":"Home","text":"Calculate view factors, illumination, and centroids for radiosity and thermal analysis.\n\n(Image: View Factors)\n\nView Factors and More","title":"View Factors Analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Overview","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Private-Functions","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Private Functions"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.AbstractAccel","page":"Home","text":"AbstractAccel\n\nMutable acceleration structure for ray/geometry intersection queries.\n\nConcrete implementations:\n\nRaycore.TLAS — software BVH/TLAS, runs on any KernelAbstractions backend.\nMantle.HWTLAS — the GPU's hardware acceleration structure, on Vulkan and Metal.\n\nMutation API\n\npush!(accel, mesh, transform): add geometry, return a TLASHandle.\ndelete!(accel, handle), update_transforms!(accel, handle, transforms), set_visible!(accel, handle, visible), set_opaque!(accel, handle, opaque).\ninstancecount(accel, handle) and instanceranges(accel), on which update_transform!(accel, handle, transform) and update_instance_transforms!(accel, transforms, n, first) are written once. An unknown or deleted handle is an ArgumentError everywhere.\n\nLifecycle\n\nsync!(accel) — sole owner of accel.static_tlas. Rebuilds in place where possible; reassigns when a buffer had to grow. No-op on a clean accel. Does NOT block the CPU on a GPU fence; backend-internal timeline tracking handles the \"still in flight\" case.\nAdapt.adapt(backend, accel) === accel.static_tlas between sync!s. Consumers re-read accel.static_tlas (or call Adapt.adapt) per dispatch. Caching the adapted form across mutations is a contract violation.\n\nQuery\n\nclosest_hit_record(adapted, ray, mask = 0xff) -> HitRecord and any_hit_record(adapted, ray, mask = 0xff) -> HitRecord: what every adapted structure implements, with hit_primitive(adapted, rec) and miss_primitive(adapted). A HitRecord is small enough to queue and expand later.\ntransparent_hits(adapted, ray, mask, Val(N), limit) -> TransparentHits{N}: the nearest N hits on instances that are not opaque, until an opaque one blocks the ray. Closest and any hits take every instance as opaque.\nclosest_hit(adapted, ray, mask = 0xff) -> (hit, tri, t, bary, instance_id)\nany_hit(adapted, ray, mask = 0xff) -> (hit, tri, t, bary, instance_id) — the SAME shape as closest_hit, not a Bool. Only the traversal differs: it stops at the first accepted hit, so t/tri are an intersection, not the nearest. Both are derived from the records once, for every structure.\nmask is the ray's cull mask: an instance is seen only when its mask (instance_mask at push!, or the record's mask) shares a bit with it. Low 8 bits, on every implementation.\nThe hit means the same on every implementation: tri is the hit triangle in world space (moved by its instance's transform), instance_id the instance's custom index (instance_id at push!, or the record's id), low 24 bits; the empty triangle and 0 on a miss.\nworld_bound(accel), n_instances(accel), n_geometries(accel).\n\nInstances a kernel writes\n\ninstance_buffer(accel, handle) — the batch's InstanceRecords, a device array a kernel may write; refit!(accel) commits what it wrote.\n\nFlush\n\nwait_for_gpu!(accel) — block CPU until all pending GPU work on this accel's queue has completed. Convenience for tear-down and benchmark isolation. Not part of the hot path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.AbstractAccel"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.BLAS","page":"Home","text":"BLAS{NodeArray, TriArray}\n\nBottom-Level Acceleration Structure - BVH over triangle geometry.\n\nType parameters allow CPU (Vector) or GPU (CuArray, ROCArray) storage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.BLAS"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.BLAS4","page":"Home","text":"BLAS4{NodeArray, TriArray}\n\nBottom-Level Acceleration Structure using BVH4 nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.BLAS4"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.BLASDescriptor","page":"Home","text":"BLASDescriptor\n\nLightweight descriptor for a BLAS in flat-array layout. Instead of storing device pointers to per-BLAS arrays (which fail on Metal when stored in GPU buffers), this stores offsets into concatenated flat arrays.\n\nFields:\n\nnodes_offset: 0-based offset into the flat allblasnodes array\nprimitives_offset: 0-based offset into the flat allblasprims array\nroot_aabb: Bounding box of the BLAS in local space\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.BLASDescriptor"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.BVHNode2","page":"Home","text":"BVHNode2\n\nCompact BVH node for two-child binary trees. Stores AABBs for both children inline (BVH2IL layout from RadeonRays).\n\nFields:\n\naabb0_min, aabb0_max: Child 0 bounding box (or vertex data for leaves)\naabb1_min, aabb1_max: Child 1 bounding box (or unused for leaves)\nchild0: Child 0 index (INVALID_NODE for leaves)\nchild1: Child 1 index (or primitive index for leaves)\nparent: Parent node index\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.BVHNode2"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.BVHNode4","page":"Home","text":"BVHNode4\n\n4-wide BVH node optimized for GPU traversal. Stores up to 4 children with their AABBs inline.\n\nMemory layout: 128 bytes (one cache line on most GPUs)\n\n4 child indices: 16 bytes\n4 AABBs: 96 bytes (24 bytes each)\nMetadata: 16 bytes\n\nLeaf nodes indicated by child_count == 0 and first primitive index in child0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.BVHNode4"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.CollisionResult","page":"Home","text":"CollisionResult{C, B}\n\nResult of collision detection, containing contact pairs and a reusable cache buffer.\n\nFields:\n\ncontacts: Vector of ContactPair (or similar) on the compute backend\nnum_contacts: Number of valid contacts\ncache: Internal buffer for reuse across frames via cache keyword\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.CollisionResult"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.ContactPair","page":"Home","text":"ContactPair\n\nA pair of contacting instances in a TLAS, identified by their 1-based instance indices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.ContactPair"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.FastClosure","page":"Home","text":"FastClosure{F, Args<:Tuple}\n\nA callable wrapper that validates a function and arguments are GPU-safe:\n\nFunction f has no captured variables (no Core.Box fields)\nArguments don't exceed compiler limits (tuple length, type depth)\n\nWhen called, appends stored args to the call: fc(x) == fc.f(x, fc.args...)\n\nThis is used internally by for_unrolled, map_unrolled, and reduce_unrolled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.FastClosure"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.HitRecord","page":"Home","text":"HitRecord\n\nWhat closest_hit_record and any_hit_record found, small enough to keep in a queue and expand later with hit_primitive.\n\nkind is HIT_MISS, HIT_TRIANGLE or HIT_GENERATED (an intersection a procedural payload committed). t is the hit's distance along the ray. For a triangle a[1] and a[2] are the barycentrics of its second and third vertex; for a generated hit a is the payload's running best, packed. instance is the instance's index in build order and primitive the primitive's index in its BLAS, both 0-based; custom is the instance's custom index (instance_id at push!, the record's id), low 24 bits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.HitRecord"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.InstanceDescriptor","page":"Home","text":"InstanceDescriptor\n\nDescribes an instance of a bottom-level BVH in world space.\n\nFields:\n\nblas_index: Index into BLAS array\ninstance_id: the instance's custom index, which closest_hit / any_hit return as their 5th value, its low 24 bits, as the hardware structures do (Vulkan's gl_InstanceCustomIndexEXT is 24 bits wide). Hikari uses it as a medium_interface_idx override, 0 meaning \"inherit from the triangle's per-face metadata\", so N instances of one BLAS can have distinct materials / media / emission without duplicating the BLAS geometry.\ntransform: Local-to-world transform (Vulkan row-major 3×4, Mat3x4f)\ninv_transform: World-to-local transform (Vulkan row-major 3×4, Mat3x4f)\nflags: Instance flags. INSTANCE_HIDDEN keeps the instance out of every ray's way (set_visible!); INSTANCE_NON_OPAQUE makes transparent_hits record its hits instead of stopping at them (opaque at push!, set_opaque!); the other bits are reserved.\nmask: the instance's cull mask. A ray traced with mask m sees the instance only when mask & m != 0; see closest_hit. Only the low 8 bits count, as on the hardware structures. The 5-argument constructor gives 0xff, which every ray sees.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.InstanceDescriptor"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.InstanceRecord","page":"Home","text":"InstanceRecord(transform, id, mask)\n\nOne instance of a geometry in a top-level structure, in the form a kernel writes it: the array instance_buffer returns holds these, and refit! is what makes the structure take what was written. The same on Raycore.TLAS and on the hardware structures.\n\ntransform: object to world, a Mat3x4f (a Mat4f is converted).\nid: the instance's custom index, which closest_hit and any_hit return as their fifth value. The low 24 bits count (Vulkan's instanceCustomIndex is 24 bits wide).\nmask: the cull mask; a ray traced with mask m sees the instance only when mask & m != 0. The low 8 bits count.\n\nThe same type on every backend. Which geometry a record instances is not in it: every record of a batch instances the geometry the batch was pushed with, and the structure writes its own descriptor from that (an InstanceDescriptor on Raycore.TLAS, a device address on Vulkan, an index on Metal).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.InstanceRecord"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.MultiTypeSet","page":"Home","text":"MultiTypeSet(backend)\n\nMutable heterogeneous vector that builds GPU-ready structures on each push!. Takes a KernelAbstractions backend at construction.\n\nExample\n\nbackend = OpenCL.OpenCLBackend()\ndhv = MultiTypeSet(backend)\ntexture = rand(Float32, 20, 20)\nidx1 = push!(dhv, MatteMaterial(texture))\nidx2 = push!(dhv, GlassMaterial(1.5f0))\n\n# Access the GPU-ready StaticMultiTypeSet directly\ngpu_smv = dhv.static  # Always up-to-date, no adapt needed\n\nPush converts arrays to TextureRefs and stores texture data as GPU arrays. The static field is rebuilt on each push to stay up-to-date.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.MultiTypeSet"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.RTHitResult","page":"Home","text":"RTHitResult\n\nRay hit output from hardware RT dispatch. 32 bytes.\n\ninstance_custom_index — value of gl_InstanceCustomIndexEXT at the hit. Under the current semantics this carries the InstanceDescriptor.instance_id (the interface-override slot).  0 means \"inherit from triangle metadata\".\ninstance_id — value of gl_InstanceID at the hit (0-based instance array position).  Used by the caller to look up per-instance data such as the BLAS triangle offset.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.RTHitResult"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.RTRay","page":"Home","text":"RTRay\n\nRay input for hardware RT dispatch. 32 bytes, matches Vulkan/Metal layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.RTRay"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.SetKey","page":"Home","text":"SetKey\n\nIndex into a heterogeneous vector, encoding both which type slot (1-based) and the index within that type's array.\n\ntype_idx: Which tuple slot (1-based), 0 = invalid/constant sentinel\nvec_idx: 1-based index within the vector at that slot\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.SetKey"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.SetView","page":"Home","text":"SetView{DataSlots, TextureSlots}\n\nSome of the slots of a StaticMultiTypeSet: the data slots numbered DataSlots and the texture slots numbered TextureSlots (tuples of the set's own slot numbers), so a SetKey and a texture's (slot, idx) mean what they mean in the set. with_index and with_texture call f(nothing, args...) for a slot the view does not hold.\n\nA kernel that reads a few of a set's slots takes a view of those: its type then names those slots only, and a slot added to the set for something else leaves it, and the kernel compiled for it, as they were. Take one with setview.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.SetView"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.StaticMultiTypeSet","page":"Home","text":"StaticMultiTypeSet{Data, Textures}\n\nImmutable heterogeneous collection with separate texture storage.\n\ndata: Tuple of GPU vectors for materials/objects\ntextures: Tuple of GPU vectors containing isbits device pointers\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.StaticMultiTypeSet"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.StaticTLAS","page":"Home","text":"StaticTLAS{NodeArray, InstArray, BLASNodeArray, BLASPrimArray, DescArray}\n\nImmutable Top-Level Acceleration Structure for GPU kernel traversal. This is what Adapt.adapt_structure returns from a TLAS.\n\nUses flat arrays with offset-based indexing instead of per-BLAS pointer arrays. This avoids the Metal issue where device pointers stored in GPU buffers cannot be reliably dereferenced by kernels.\n\nThe struct is immutable and contains only the arrays needed for ray traversal. No management state (dictionaries, free lists, etc.) - those stay on CPU in TLAS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.StaticTLAS"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.TLAS","page":"Home","text":"TLAS{Backend}\n\nMutable Top-Level Acceleration Structure with direct GPU arrays.\n\nGPU-first design: instances are appended directly to GPU array using efficient GPU append. CPU-side dictionary provides O(1) handle lookups.\n\nAdapted-form invariant (READ THIS)\n\nsync!(tlas) is the single owner of the GPU-adapted form. It rebuilds as efficiently as possible — in place via resize!/copyto! where the backing buffer can be reused, freshly allocated only when a buffer grew — and stores the result in tlas.static_tlas. sync! MAY reassign tlas.static_tlas = new_static_tlas when a buffer was reallocated.\n\nEvery consumer that hands an accel to a raytracing kernel MUST go through tlas.static_tlas or Adapt.adapt(backend, tlas) (which reads / refreshes tlas.static_tlas) per dispatch. Those are cheap; sync! did the heavy lifting. NEVER cache the StaticTLAS returned by adapt across mutations — after a reshape-driven reallocation the cached snapshot holds a stale device pointer.\n\nThe same contract applies to HWTLAS — see its docstring.\n\nType Parameters\n\nBackend: KernelAbstractions backend (CPU(), LavaBackend(), CUDABackend(), etc.)\n\nFields\n\nbackend: KernelAbstractions backend for kernels\nnodes: BVH nodes array (on backend, grown in place on sync! where possible)\ninstances: Instance descriptors array (GPU array, direct append)\nblas_array: BLAS objects array (GPU array with isbits pointers)\nroot_aabb: World-space bounding box\nhandle_to_range: Handle -> range in instances array (CPU-side)\ndeleted_handles: Handles deleted but not yet compacted (CPU-side)\nrecords: Handle -> the batch's InstanceRecords, a backend array a kernel  may write (instance_buffer); refit! copies them into  instances. Live handles only.\nblas_storage: Per-BLAS backing arrays; keeps GPU buffers alive for isbits pointers\nstatic_tlas: GPU-adapted form, owned by sync!. Consumers read this, do  not cache it across mutations.\ndirty: Whether BVH topology needs rebuild\ntransforms_dirty: Whether instance transforms changed and the TLAS needs refit\nrevision: Monotonic counter bumped by every mutation that reshapes  GPU-visible arrays. Intended purely as a diagnostic signal — consumers that  go through tlas.static_tlas don't need to check it.\n\nUsage\n\ntlas = TLAS(CPU())\nh1 = push!(tlas, mesh)\nh2 = push!(tlas, mesh, transforms)\nupdate_transform!(tlas, h2, new_transform)\ndelete!(tlas, h1)\nsync!(tlas)                    # Rebuild / refresh tlas.static_tlas\nstatic = adapt(backend, tlas)  # Reads tlas.static_tlas — cheap, call per dispatch\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.TLAS"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.TLAS-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:GeometryBasics.Mesh}}","page":"Home","text":"TLAS(meshes::AbstractVector{<:GeometryBasics.Mesh}; backend=KA.CPU()) -> (TLAS, Vector{TLASHandle})\n\nCreate a mutable TLAS from a vector of GB.Mesh objects. Each mesh becomes a BLAS with a single instance at identity transform.\n\nReturns the mutable TLAS and a vector of TLASHandles for later reference.\n\nExamples\n\ntlas, handles = TLAS([floor_mesh, wall_mesh, sphere_mesh])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.TLAS"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.TLAS-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"TLAS(backend; primtype = Triangle{UInt32}) -> TLAS\n\nCreate an empty TLAS for the given backend. Use push! to add geometries/instances, then sync! to rebuild the BVH. Adapt.adapt_structure returns a StaticTLAS for kernel traversal.\n\nprimtype is the primitive type the structure holds while it holds nothing: pass the Triangle{Meta} your pushes will carry, so an empty scene and a populated one compile to the same kernel.\n\nExample\n\ntlas = TLAS(OpenCLBackend())\nh1 = push!(tlas, geometry)\nh2 = push!(tlas, Instance(geometry, transforms))\nsync!(tlas)  # Rebuild BVH on backend\nstatic = adapt(backend, tlas)  # StaticTLAS with isbits pointers for kernels\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.TLAS"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.TLAS-Union{Tuple{P}, Tuple{AbstractVector{P}, Function}} where P","page":"Home","text":"TLAS(primitives::AbstractVector, metadata_fn::Function; backend=KA.CPU())\n\nUniversal TLAS constructor. Each primitive (GB.Mesh or AbstractGeometry) becomes a BLAS with a single instance.\n\nEach mesh is automatically treated as an instance at identity transform. Perfect for scenes where you just have different meshes and want automatic instancing.\n\nGPU-first: Specify backend to build all BLASes directly on GPU.\n\nExample:\n\ngeometries = [cat_mesh, floor, sphere]\ntlas = TLAS(geometries, (mesh_idx, tri_idx) -> UInt32(mesh_idx))\n\n# GPU-first:\ntlas = TLAS(geometries, metadata_fn; backend=OpenCLBackend())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.TLAS"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.TLAS4","page":"Home","text":"TLAS4{NodeArray, InstArray, BLASArray}\n\nTop-Level Acceleration Structure using BVH4 nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.TLAS4"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.TLASHandle","page":"Home","text":"TLASHandle\n\nStable handle for referencing instances in a TLAS. Simple unique ID for O(1) lookup in handletorange dictionary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.TLASHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.TransparentHits","page":"Home","text":"TransparentHits{N}\n\nWhat transparent_hits found along a ray.\n\nhits: the nearest min(count, N) hits on instances that are not opaque, nearest first.\ncount: how many such hits were found, each primitive once. A structure may stop looking once it has N; so when count reaches N, hits past hits[N] may exist: trace on from hits[N].t.\nblocked: the ray met an opaque instance, or more than limit others were found.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.TransparentHits"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.any_hit-Tuple{AbstractAdaptedAccel, Raycore.AbstractRay, Integer}","page":"Home","text":"any_hit(adapted, ray, mask = 0xff) -> (hit, primitive, t, bary, instance_id)\n\nThe SAME shape as closest_hit, not a Bool: only the traversal differs, stopping at the first accepted hit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.any_hit"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.any_hit4-Union{Tuple{R}, Tuple{BLAS4, R}} where R<:Raycore.AbstractRay","page":"Home","text":"any_hit4(blas::BLAS4, ray::AbstractRay) -> (hit, primitive, distance, barycentric)\n\nTraverse BVH4 to find any intersection (early exit).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.any_hit4"},{"category":"function","location":"index.html#Raycore.any_hit_record","page":"Home","text":"any_hit_record(adapted, ray, mask = 0xff) -> HitRecord\n\nAn intersection of the ray, the first traversal accepts. Every adapted structure implements the three-argument method; any_hit is derived from it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.any_hit_record"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.any_hit_record-Tuple{StaticTLAS, Raycore.AbstractRay, Integer}","page":"Home","text":"any_hit_record(tlas::StaticTLAS, ray::AbstractRay, mask) -> HitRecord\n\nAn intersection of the ray, the first the traversal finds, every instance taken as opaque. Faster than closest_hit_record when only occlusion matters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.any_hit_record"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.apply_instance_records_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"GPU kernel: the records of one batch, as a kernel may have written them, into its instances: record i becomes instance first_idx + i - 1's transform, its inverse, its id and its mask. The instance keeps its geometry and its flags.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.apply_instance_records_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.build_blas-Union{Tuple{AbstractVector{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:Triangle","page":"Home","text":"build_blas(primitives) -> BLAS\n\nBuild a Bottom-Level Acceleration Structure using Linear BVH (LBVH).\n\nUses KernelAbstractions for automatic CPU/GPU execution based on input array type.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\nCompute scene AABB\nCalculate Morton codes in parallel (GPU kernel)\nSort primitives by Morton code\nBuild binary radix tree in parallel (GPU kernel)\nCompute AABBs bottom-up\n\nBased on Karras 2012 \"Maximizing Parallelism in the Construction of BVHs, Octrees, and k-d Trees\"\n\nArguments\n\nprimitives: Vector or GPU array of Triangle objects (array type determines backend)\n\nExample\n\n# CPU execution\nblas_cpu = build_blas(triangles)  # Vector{Triangle}\n\n# GPU execution (CUDA)\nusing CUDA\ngpu_triangles = CuArray(triangles)\nblas_gpu = build_blas(gpu_triangles)  # CuArray{Triangle}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.build_blas"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.build_blas4-Union{Tuple{AbstractVector{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:Triangle","page":"Home","text":"build_blas4(primitives) -> BLAS4\n\nBuild a BLAS using BVH4 nodes for faster traversal.\n\nBuild standard LBVH (BVH2) using existing kernels\nCollapse BVH2 -> BVH4\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.build_blas4"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.build_tlas-Union{Tuple{B}, Tuple{AbstractVector{B}, AbstractVector{InstanceDescriptor}}} where B<:BLAS","page":"Home","text":"build_tlas(blas_array::AbstractVector{BLAS}, instances::AbstractVector{InstanceDescriptor}) -> StaticTLAS\n\nBuild a Top-Level Acceleration Structure over instances. Uses LBVH over transformed instance AABBs.\n\nReturns a StaticTLAS with flat BLAS arrays suitable for ray traversal. Uses KernelAbstractions for automatic CPU/GPU execution based on input array type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.build_tlas"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.build_topology_for_node-Tuple{Int32, AbstractVector{UInt32}, Int32}","page":"Home","text":"Build topology for one internal node. Regular Julia function for testability.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.build_topology_for_node"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.build_triangle-NTuple{6, Any}","page":"Home","text":"build_triangle(vertices, normals, uvs, indices, face_idx, metadata)\n\nBuild a Triangle from decomposed mesh arrays at the given face index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.build_triangle"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.calculate_morton_code_for_prim-Tuple{Int64, AbstractVector{<:Triangle}, GeometryBasics.Point{3, Float32}, GeometryBasics.Vec{3, Float32}}","page":"Home","text":"Calculate Morton code for a single primitive. This is a regular Julia function, callable from CPU or GPU.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.calculate_morton_code_for_prim"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.calculate_tlas_morton_code-Tuple{Int64, AbstractVector{InstanceDescriptor}, AbstractVector, GeometryBasics.Point{3, Float32}, GeometryBasics.Vec{3, Float32}}","page":"Home","text":"Calculate Morton code for a single instance centroid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.calculate_tlas_morton_code"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.closest_hit-Tuple{AbstractAdaptedAccel, Raycore.AbstractRay, Integer}","page":"Home","text":"closest_hit(adapted, ray, mask = 0xff) -> (hit, primitive, t, bary, instance_id)\n\nThe nearest intersection, expanded: primitive is the hit triangle in world space and instance_id the instance's custom index; the empty primitive and 0 on a miss. The same on every adapted structure, software and hardware.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.closest_hit"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.closest_hit4-Union{Tuple{R}, Tuple{BLAS4, R}} where R<:Raycore.AbstractRay","page":"Home","text":"closest_hit4(blas::BLAS4, ray::AbstractRay) -> (hit, primitive, distance, barycentric)\n\nTraverse BVH4 to find closest intersection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.closest_hit4"},{"category":"function","location":"index.html#Raycore.closest_hit_record","page":"Home","text":"closest_hit_record(adapted, ray, mask = 0xff) -> HitRecord\n\nThe ray's nearest intersection. Every adapted structure implements the three-argument method; closest_hit is derived from it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.closest_hit_record"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.closest_hit_record-Tuple{StaticTLAS, Raycore.AbstractRay, Integer}","page":"Home","text":"closest_hit_record(tlas::StaticTLAS, ray::AbstractRay, mask) -> HitRecord\n\nThe ray's closest intersection, every instance taken as opaque; mask is the ray's cull mask (see traverse).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.closest_hit_record"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.collide_instances-Tuple{TLAS}","page":"Home","text":"collide_instances(tlas::TLAS; cache=nothing) -> CollisionResult\n\nFind all pairs of instances whose world-space AABBs overlap.\n\nThis is a broad-phase test — it identifies which instances might be in contact based on their bounding boxes. For exact triangle-triangle contact, use collide.\n\nReturns a CollisionResult with ContactPairs (1-indexed instance IDs).\n\nExample\n\ntlas = TLAS(backend)\npush!(tlas, mesh_a, transform_a)\npush!(tlas, mesh_b, transform_b)\nsync!(tlas)\n\nresult = collide_instances(tlas)\nfor i in 1:result.num_contacts\n    pair = result.contacts[i]\n    println(\"Instance $(pair.instance_a) overlaps instance $(pair.instance_b)\")\nend\n\n# Reuse buffers for next frame:\nresult2 = collide_instances(tlas; cache=result.cache)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.collide_instances"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.collide_instances_any-Tuple{TLAS, TLASHandle, TLASHandle}","page":"Home","text":"collide_instances_any(tlas::TLAS, handle_a::TLASHandle, handle_b::TLASHandle) -> Bool\n\nTest whether two specific instance groups overlap (broad-phase AABB test). Fast early-exit — returns true on first overlap found.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.collide_instances_any"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.copyto_texture!-Union{Tuple{AT}, Tuple{MultiTypeSet, TextureRef{AT}, AbstractArray}} where AT","page":"Home","text":"copyto_texture!(dhv, ref, data)\n\nWrite data into the GPU texture addressed by ref.  Same-size is a plain copyto! (device pointer unchanged — no other state needs updating).  A vector of another length goes through Base.resize!, which a backend may make capacity-aware (no reallocation within capacity, the old buffer retired once the GPU is past it). Any other shape gets a fresh array of the new shape in the old one's place: Base has no resize! for more than one dimension, and a backend that adds one is not something every backend does.\n\nIf the device pointer actually moved (pool-alloc returned a fresh buffer), the one affected slot of static.textures[AT_slot] is updated via a single scalar setindex! — no re-adapt of the whole table, no per-frame leak.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.copyto_texture!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.create_leaf_for_prim-Tuple{Int64, AbstractVector{<:Triangle}, BVHNode2, Int32}","page":"Home","text":"Create leaf node for one primitive. Regular Julia function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.create_leaf_for_prim"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.create_tlas_leaf_for_instance-Tuple{Int64, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{InstanceDescriptor}, AbstractVector, UInt32}","page":"Home","text":"Create TLAS leaf node for one instance (stores world-space AABB, not triangle vertices).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.create_tlas_leaf_for_instance"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.deref-Union{Tuple{AT}, Tuple{MultiTypeSet, TextureRef{AT}}} where AT","page":"Home","text":"Resolve a host set's texture reference to its owning array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.deref"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.empty_triangle-Union{Tuple{Type{Triangle{TMeta}}}, Tuple{TMeta}} where TMeta","page":"Home","text":"empty_triangle(::Type{Triangle{TMeta}}) -> Triangle{TMeta}\n\nZero-initialized Triangle suitable as a no-hit sentinel. All vertex, normal, tangent, and uv components are 0; metadata is field-wise zero-constructed (works for any POD struct whose primitive fields have zero(T) defined).\n\nTakes the full Triangle type (not just the metadata type) so callers can write empty_triangle(eltype(triangles)) directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.empty_triangle"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.for_unrolled-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Tuple, Vararg{Any}}} where F","page":"Home","text":"for_unrolled(f, tuple, args...)\n\nIterate over tuple at compile-time, calling f(element, args...) for each element. No return value (use for side effects).\n\nThe function f must not capture any variables - pass all data as args instead.\n\nExample\n\nlights = (sun_light, point_light, spot_light)\ntotal = Ref(RGBSpectrum(0f0))\n\n# Bad - captures `total` and `ray`:\nfor light in lights\n    total[] += le(light, ray)  # Boxing on GPU!\nend\n\n# Good - pass as arguments:\nfor_unrolled(add_light!, lights, total, ray)\n# Where: add_light!(light, total, ray) = total[] += le(light, ray)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.for_unrolled"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.for_unrolled-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Val{N}, Vararg{Any}}} where {F, N}","page":"Home","text":"for_unrolled(f, ::Val{N}, args...)\n\nIterate from 1 to N at compile-time, calling f(i, args...) for each index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.for_unrolled"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.free!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"free!(x)\n\nTrigger the registered finalizer on x to release GPU memory. Safe to call on any object — no-op if no finalizer is registered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.free!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.free!-Tuple{MultiTypeSet}","page":"Home","text":"free!(set::MultiTypeSet)\n\nRelease GPU memory held by the MultiTypeSet — the shadow-owned texture arrays plus the static material/texture slot buffers.  Does not synchronize.\n\nPrecondition (caller's responsibility): the GPU must be idle for set.backend before this is called.  MultiTypeSet.texture_gpu_arrays is a shadow-ownership site: the only references from GPU work are raw BDAs in arg buffers, so nothing inside free! can prove it's safe to finalize — the caller establishes that, typically by calling sync! on the enclosing accel / scene (which synchronizes its backend) or by returning from a colorbuffer that completed with device_wait_idle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.free!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.free!-Tuple{TLAS}","page":"Home","text":"free!(tlas::TLAS)\n\nRelease all GPU memory held by tlas.  Does not synchronize.\n\nPrecondition (caller's responsibility): the GPU must be idle for tlas.backend before this is called — either because the caller just returned from sync!(tlas) / Hikari.sync!(scene), from a colorbuffer that issued its own device_wait_idle, or because the caller has otherwise issued KA.synchronize(tlas.backend).\n\nCalling free! while dispatches are still in flight through a LavaArray / VkAccelerationStructureKHR BDA captured in an arg buffer is a use-after-free.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.free!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.get_child4-Tuple{BVHNode4, Int64}","page":"Home","text":"Get child index by position (0-3).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.get_child4"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.get_child_aabb4-Tuple{BVHNode4, Int64}","page":"Home","text":"Get child AABB by position (1-4).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.get_child_aabb4"},{"category":"function","location":"index.html#Raycore.get_instance","page":"Home","text":"get_instance(tlas::TLAS, handle::TLASHandle) -> InstanceDescriptor\nget_instance(tlas::TLAS, handle::TLASHandle, instance_idx::Integer) -> InstanceDescriptor\n\nRetrieve the InstanceDescriptor for a handle. If the handle has multiple instances (created with multiple transforms), use instance_idx to specify which one (1-based).\n\nNote: Reads from GPU array, may involve a device-to-host copy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.get_instance"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.get_instances-Tuple{TLAS, TLASHandle}","page":"Home","text":"get_instances(tlas::TLAS, handle::TLASHandle) -> Vector{InstanceDescriptor}\n\nRetrieve all InstanceDescriptors for a handle (for handles with multiple transforms).\n\nNote: Reads from GPU array, involves a device-to-host copy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.get_instances"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.getindex_unrolled-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Tuple, Int32, Vararg{Any}}} where F","page":"Home","text":"getindex_unrolled(f, tuple, idx::Int32, args...) -> result\n\nSelect element at runtime index idx from tuple and apply f(element, args...). Uses unrolled if-branches for GPU compatibility - no dynamic dispatch.\n\nThe index is 1-based. If idx is out of bounds, returns f(tuple[1], args...) as fallback.\n\nExample\n\nlights = (sun_light, point_light, env_light)\nlight_idx = Int32(2)\n\n# Sample from the selected light\nsample = getindex_unrolled(sample_light, lights, light_idx, point, lambda, u)\n# Equivalent to: sample_light(point_light, point, lambda, u)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.getindex_unrolled"},{"category":"function","location":"index.html#Raycore.hit_primitive","page":"Home","text":"hit_primitive(adapted, rec::HitRecord) -> primitive\n\nThe primitive rec hit, in world space (moved by its instance's transform). Every adapted structure implements it, for the records it returns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.hit_primitive"},{"category":"function","location":"index.html#Raycore.instance_buffer","page":"Home","text":"instance_buffer(tlas, handle::TLASHandle) -> device vector of InstanceRecord\n\nThe device array holding the InstanceRecords of the batch handle names, in the order of its instances: the array the batch was pushed with when it was pushed with one, else the one push! made. A kernel may write it — new transforms, ids or masks — and refit!(tlas) is what makes the structure take what was written.\n\nThe array can be longer than the batch: only its first n_instances records are instances. Throws an ArgumentError for a handle that names no batch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.instance_buffer"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.instance_buffer-Tuple{TLAS, TLASHandle}","page":"Home","text":"instance_buffer(tlas::TLAS, handle::TLASHandle) -> backend vector of InstanceRecord\n\nThe records of the batch handle names, one per instance in push order: the array push! made, on the structure's backend. See instance_buffer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.instance_buffer"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.is_degenerate_face-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"is_degenerate_face(vertices, indices, face_idx)\n\nCheck if a triangle face is degenerate (zero area) without constructing a full Triangle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.is_degenerate_face"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.is_interior4-Tuple{BVHNode4}","page":"Home","text":"Check if node is interior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.is_interior4"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.is_leaf4-Tuple{BVHNode4}","page":"Home","text":"Check if node is a leaf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.is_leaf4"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.is_valid-Tuple{TLAS, TLASHandle}","page":"Home","text":"is_valid(tlas::TLAS, handle::TLASHandle) -> Bool\n\nCheck if a handle is still valid (not deleted). O(1) operation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.is_valid"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.map_unrolled-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Tuple, Vararg{Any}}} where F","page":"Home","text":"map_unrolled(f, tuple, args...) -> Tuple\n\nTransform each element of tuple at compile-time, returning a new tuple. Calls f(element, args...) for each element.\n\nExample\n\nlights = (sun_light, point_light)\ncontributions = map_unrolled(compute_light, lights, hit_point, normal)\n# Returns: (compute_light(sun_light, hit_point, normal),\n#           compute_light(point_light, hit_point, normal))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.map_unrolled"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.n_geometries-Tuple{TLAS}","page":"Home","text":"n_geometries(tlas::TraversableTLAS)\n\nReturn number of unique BLAS geometries in the TLAS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.n_geometries"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.n_instances-Tuple{StaticTLAS}","page":"Home","text":"n_instances(tlas::TraversableTLAS)\n\nReturn total number of live instance descriptors in the TLAS.\n\nFor a TLAS, instances delete!d but not yet compacted by sync! are excluded; the count tracks what the next sync! will publish, not the backing buffer length. StaticTLAS has no pending state, so it returns length(tlas.instances) directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.n_instances"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.n_instances-Tuple{TLAS, TLASHandle}","page":"Home","text":"n_instances(tlas::TLAS, handle::TLASHandle) -> Int\n\nGet the number of instances referenced by a handle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.n_instances"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.n_total_instances-Tuple{TLAS}","page":"Home","text":"n_total_instances(tlas::TLAS) -> Int\n\nGet the total number of active instances in the TLAS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.n_total_instances"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.reduce_unrolled-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Tuple, Any, Vararg{Any}}} where F","page":"Home","text":"reduce_unrolled(f, tuple, init, args...) -> result\n\nReduce tuple at compile-time using f(accumulator, element, args...).\n\nExample\n\nlights = (sun_light, point_light, env_light)\n\n# Compute total light contribution\ntotal = reduce_unrolled(add_light_contribution, lights, RGBSpectrum(0f0), ray, hit_point)\n# Where: add_light_contribution(acc, light, ray, hp) = acc + compute_li(light, ray, hp)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.reduce_unrolled"},{"category":"function","location":"index.html#Raycore.refit!","page":"Home","text":"refit!(tlas) -> tlas\nrefit!(blas, vertices) -> blas\n\nBring an acceleration structure up to date with geometry that moved, keeping its topology.\n\nrefit!(tlas) takes every instance record as it is now — a kernel may have written them through instance_buffer — and refits the structure over them in place. A structure that cannot be refit (its batches changed since it was built) is rebuilt instead. The adapted form a kernel traces stays the same object across a refit, so a plan holding it sees the new instances.\n\nrefit!(blas, vertices) moves the vertices of a bottom-level structure built with allow_update = true and refits it in place: same vertex count, same triangles. A structure built without allow_update, or a different vertex count, is an ArgumentError. A top-level structure stores the bounds of what it instances, so refit! every TLAS that instances the BLAS afterwards.\n\nA refit keeps the tree the structure was built with, so traversal slows as the geometry moves away from that pose; rebuild a structure that moves far.\n\nNot exported: Mantle exports a refit! of its own, for plans, and the two would make the bare name ambiguous wherever both packages are usinged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.refit!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.refit!-Tuple{TLAS}","page":"Home","text":"refit!(tlas::TLAS) -> TLAS\n\nTake every batch's instance records as they are now, wherever they were written from — update_transform!, or a kernel writing instance_buffer — into the instances (transform, its inverse, id and mask; each instance keeps its geometry and its visibility), and refit the BVH over them; a structure whose topology changed is rebuilt instead. See refit! for the contract every acceleration structure shares.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.refit!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.reflect-Tuple{GeometryBasics.Vec{3, Float32}, GeometryBasics.Vec{3, Float32}}","page":"Home","text":"reflect(wo, n) -> wi\n\nwo reflected about n: -wo + 2 (wo ⋅ n) n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.reflect"},{"category":"function","location":"index.html#Raycore.set_opaque!","page":"Home","text":"set_opaque!(accel, handle::TLASHandle, opaque::Bool) -> Bool\n\nWhether transparent_hits stops at the instances of handle (true, the default at push!) or records their hits and goes on. Closest and any hits treat every instance as opaque either way. Returns false for a handle that names nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.set_opaque!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.set_parents_for_node-Tuple{Int32, AbstractVector{BVHNode2}}","page":"Home","text":"Set parent pointers for one node's children. Regular Julia function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.set_parents_for_node"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.set_visible!-Tuple{TLAS, TLASHandle, Bool}","page":"Home","text":"set_visible!(accel, handle::TLASHandle, visible::Bool) -> Bool\n\nShow or hide every instance of handle. A hidden instance stays in the structure, with its geometry, its index and its transform; no ray hits it. Showing it again costs no rebuild of its geometry. Returns false for a handle that names nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.set_visible!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.setview-Union{Tuple{M}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{StaticMultiTypeSet, NTuple{N, Int64}, NTuple{M, Int64}}} where {N, M}","page":"Home","text":"setview(smv::StaticMultiTypeSet, data_slots::Tuple, texture_slots::Tuple) -> SetView\n\nThe data slots data_slots and the texture slots texture_slots of smv, by their numbers in it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.setview"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.similar_soa-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, Type{T}, Any}} where T","page":"Home","text":"similar_soa(template_array, T::Type, num_elements) -> NamedTuple\n\nCreate a Structure of Arrays (SoA) layout for type T with num_elements entries. Uses template_array to determine the array type (Array, ROCArray, etc.).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.similar_soa"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.store!-Tuple{MultiTypeSet, SetKey, Any}","page":"Home","text":"store!(dhv::MultiTypeSet, key::SetKey, item) -> SetKey\n\nStore item in place of the item under key and return the key it is stored under now: key when item updates that item in place (update_item gives a value of its stored type, see update!), else a slot of item's own type, push!ed, with key vacated (delete!). An invalid key (nothing stored) is a push!.\n\nA material swapped for another kind of material is the second case: its type is not the slot's, so it cannot be written there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.store!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.sum_unrolled-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Tuple, Vararg{Any}}} where F","page":"Home","text":"sum_unrolled(f, tuple, args...) -> result\n\nSum f(element, args...) over all elements of tuple.\n\nExample\n\nlights = (sun_light, point_light)\ntotal = sum_unrolled(le, lights, ray)\n# Computes: le(sun_light, ray) + le(point_light, ray)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.sum_unrolled"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.sync!-Tuple{TLAS}","page":"Home","text":"sync!(tlas::TLAS) -> TLAS\n\nRebuild the BVH structure if dirty, then wait for the GPU to finish all work currently queued on tlas.backend (via KA.synchronize). No-op if already up-to-date AND no work is pending.\n\nsync! is the single owner of tlas.static_tlas. It rebuilds in place (resize!/copyto! on the same backing buffer) wherever possible so that a StaticTLAS returned by an earlier Adapt.adapt(backend, tlas) still sees the new geometry, and reallocates + reassigns tlas.static_tlas when a buffer needed to grow.\n\nAfter sync!(tlas) returns:\n\nThe TLAS reflects all prior push!/delete! calls.\ntlas.static_tlas is the fresh adapted form.\nThe GPU is idle for tlas.backend.\nAny resource that is no longer reachable through the (new) accel is safe to release synchronously: its VkAccelerationStructureKHR / BDA captures in in-flight work have drained.\n\nInvariants for callers:\n\nupdate! / push! / delete! on the TLAS do NOT wait and do NOT perform cleanup; they mark state dirty and return immediately. Call sync! to establish a safe boundary before freeing transitively- owned resources.\nConsumers of the adapted form read tlas.static_tlas (or call Adapt.adapt(backend, tlas)) per dispatch. They MUST NOT cache the returned StaticTLAS across mutations — after a reshape-driven reassign the cached snapshot holds a stale device pointer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.sync!"},{"category":"function","location":"index.html#Raycore.transparent_hits","page":"Home","text":"transparent_hits(adapted, ray, mask, Val(N), limit) -> TransparentHits{N}\n\nThe hits of ray on instances that are not opaque (opaque = false at push!, set_opaque!), the nearest N of them kept, until a hit on an opaque instance blocks the ray. Nothing decides about a hit during the traversal: a caller shades the kept hits in order, and traces on from the last of them when count >= N. Cycles' shadow rays through transparent surfaces (scene_intersect_shadow_all). Every adapted structure implements it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.transparent_hits"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.update!-Tuple{MultiTypeSet, SetKey, Any}","page":"Home","text":"update!(dhv::MultiTypeSet, key::SetKey, new_item)\n\nUpdate an existing item in the set.  The new item is walked against the stored form via update_item: existing TextureRef slots are reused (the new array data is copied into the existing GPU buffer, reallocating on size mismatch), const-Texture fields are unwrapped to their scalar values, and other fields fall through as plain value replacement.\n\nThere is deliberately no maybe_convert_field/store_texture call on this path — that would allocate a new GPU slot per update and leak hundreds of MB per frame for plots with per-vertex color textures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.update!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.update!-Tuple{TLAS, TLASHandle, Any}","page":"Home","text":"update!(tlas::TLAS, handle::TLASHandle, new_geometry)\n\nReplace the geometry (BLAS) for a handle. All instances sharing this BLAS get updated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.update!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.update_instance_transforms_offset_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"GPU kernel: the transforms of one batch, transforms[i] for its instance i, into its instance first_idx + i - 1 and its record records[i]. Each keeps its geometry, flags, id and mask.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.update_instance_transforms_offset_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.update_item-Union{Tuple{AT}, Tuple{MultiTypeSet, TextureRef{AT}, AbstractArray}} where AT","page":"Home","text":"update_item(dhv::MultiTypeSet, old, new)\n\nCompute the updated representation of old after applying new's data. Reuses existing TextureRef slots (copying new's arrays into them rather than allocating fresh GPU buffers).  Extended by backend / material packages (e.g. Hikari) with overloads for their wrapper types — notably Texture (unwraps const, routes array data to copyto_texture!) and VertexColorTexture.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.update_item"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.update_particle_materials_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"GPU kernel: Batch update materials based on particle velocities.\n\nUpdates material colors using a heat-map based on velocity magnitude. Also updates metallic/roughness with per-particle noise variation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.update_particle_materials_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.update_tlas_leaf_aabbs_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"GPU kernel: Update TLAS leaf node AABBs from instance transforms.\n\nAfter transforms are updated, this kernel recomputes world-space AABBs for all leaf nodes. Must run before the refit (refit_lbvh!).\n\nNOTE: Leaf nodes are Morton-sorted, so we must use the stored instance index (child1) to look up the correct instance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.update_tlas_leaf_aabbs_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.update_transform!-Tuple{AbstractAccel, TLASHandle, StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{4, 3, Float32, 12}}","page":"Home","text":"update_transform!(tlas::TLAS, handle::TLASHandle, transform)\n\nSet every instance of handle to transform, directly on the device. One transform per instance is update_transforms!. The hardware structures (Mantle.HWTLAS) mean the same, and RayMakie moves a mesh, a batch of one, this way on either.\n\ntransform may be a Mat4f (homogeneous 4×4) or the canonical Vulkan row-major 3×4 (Mat3x4f); the Mat4f form is converted internally via mat4_to_mat3x4. After calling this, use refit_tlas! to update the BVH AABBs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.update_transform!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.update_transforms!-Tuple{TLAS, TLASHandle, AbstractVector{StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{4, 3, Float32, 12}}}","page":"Home","text":"update_transforms!(tlas::TLAS, handle::TLASHandle, transforms)\n\nUpdate all instances' transforms in a group directly on GPU, in the instances and in the batch's records (instance_buffer) alike, keeping each instance's id and mask. Length must match the number of instances in the handle.\n\nTransforms may be Mat4f or the canonical Vulkan row-major 3×4 (Mat3x4f); Mat4f arrays are converted element-wise via mat4_to_mat3x4. The array can be CPU or GPU — it'll be adapted to the TLAS backend. The next sync! refits the BVH over them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.update_transforms!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.wait_for_gpu!-Tuple{AbstractAccel}","page":"Home","text":"wait_for_gpu!(accel::AbstractAccel)\n\nBlock the CPU until the GPU has completed all prior work that could be reading accel or its adapted form. Default implementation calls KA.synchronize on accel.backend. Concrete types that carry their own queue (e.g. Mantle.HWTLAS) override this to wait on the specific timeline.\n\nConvenience only. The per-dispatch hot path does NOT call this; see sync!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.wait_for_gpu!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.with_index-Union{Tuple{Textures}, Tuple{Data}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, StaticMultiTypeSet{Data, Textures}, SetKey, Vararg{Any}}} where {F, Data<:Tuple, Textures}","page":"Home","text":"with_index(f, smv::StaticMultiTypeSet, idx::SetKey, args...)\n\nExecute function f with the element at index idx, passing additional args. The function is called as f(element, args...) where element has a concrete type.\n\nUses a single if-elseif-else chain for SPIR-V structured control flow compatibility. The function f must not capture variables - pass all data as args.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.with_index"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.with_texture-Union{Tuple{Textures}, Tuple{Data}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, StaticMultiTypeSet{Data, Textures}, Integer, Integer, Vararg{Any}}} where {F, Data<:Tuple, Textures<:Tuple}","page":"Home","text":"with_texture(f, smv::StaticMultiTypeSet, slot::Integer, idx::Integer, args...)\n\nCall f(texture_array, args...) for the texture at a RUNTIME (slot, idx).\n\nderef indexes smv.textures[TIdx] with TIdx taken from the TextureRef TYPE, so a texture reference cannot be type-erased: two image maps in different type slots are different Julia types, and any struct holding one inherits that. For materials that is the whole constant-vs-texture type explosion.\n\nThis is the escape hatch: the slot is a runtime value and the dispatch is an if-elseif chain over the tuple, like with_index. Each arm is MONOMORPHIC, so as long as f returns the same type from every arm (sample-and-convert rather than returning the array) the call is type-stable and GPU-safe. Returning the array itself would produce a union and is not supported by design.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.with_texture"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.world_bound-Tuple{BLAS}","page":"Home","text":"Get world-space AABB of a BLAS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.world_bound"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.world_bound-Tuple{TraversableTLAS}","page":"Home","text":"Get world-space AABB of a TLAS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.world_bound"},{"category":"macro","location":"index.html#Raycore.@get-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"@get field1, field2, ... = soa[idx]\n\nMacro to extract multiple fields from a Structure of Arrays (SoA) at index idx.\n\nExample\n\nray_queue = (ray=[r1, r2, r3], pixel_x=[1, 2, 3], pixel_y=[4, 5, 6])\n@get ray, pixel_x, pixel_y = ray_queue[2]\n# Expands to:\n# ray = ray_queue.ray[2]\n# pixel_x = ray_queue.pixel_x[2]\n# pixel_y = ray_queue.pixel_y[2]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.@get"},{"category":"macro","location":"index.html#Raycore.@set-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"@set soa[idx] = (field1=val1, field2=val2, ...)\n\nMacro to set multiple fields in a Structure of Arrays (SoA) at index idx. Expects named tuple syntax on the right side.\n\nExample\n\nray_queue = (ray=Vector{Ray}(undef, 10), pixel_x=zeros(Int32, 10))\n@set ray_queue[1] = (ray=my_ray, pixel_x=Int32(5))\n# Expands to:\n# ray_queue.ray[1] = my_ray\n# ray_queue.pixel_x[1] = Int32(5)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.@set"},{"category":"constant","location":"index.html#Raycore.INSTANCE_HIDDEN","page":"Home","text":"The InstanceDescriptor.flags bit of an instance no ray hits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.INSTANCE_HIDDEN"},{"category":"constant","location":"index.html#Raycore.INSTANCE_NON_OPAQUE","page":"Home","text":"The InstanceDescriptor.flags bit of an instance whose hits transparent_hits records.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.INSTANCE_NON_OPAQUE"},{"category":"constant","location":"index.html#Raycore.TOP_LEVEL_SENTINEL","page":"Home","text":"Sentinel value to mark top-level to bottom-level transitions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.TOP_LEVEL_SENTINEL"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.BLASArrays","page":"Home","text":"BLASArrays\n\nPer-BLAS backing GPU arrays.  Field ownership transitively keeps the nodes / primitives buffers alive while the TLAS holds them, so the isbits device pointers stored in blas_array (and the flat arrays used by StaticTLAS) remain valid.\n\nprimitives is left at the bare AbstractVector bound rather than AbstractVector{<:Triangle} because differently-parameterized Triangle{TMetadata} subtypes need to coexist across BLASes; the tighter bound forces a UnionAll that doesn't help dispatch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.BLASArrays"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.BVH4Leaf","page":"Home","text":"BVH4Leaf\n\nLeaf node that stores triangle vertices directly (like BVH2IL format). For BVH4, we keep triangles separate and reference by index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.BVH4Leaf"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.BoxLeaves","page":"Home","text":"A TLAS's leaves: each holds its instance's world-space box in aabb0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.BoxLeaves"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.CollapseTask","page":"Home","text":"CollapseTask\n\nWork item for the BVH2 -> BVH4 collapse pass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.CollapseTask"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.Mat3x4f","page":"Home","text":"Mat3x4f\n\nRow-major Float32 3×4 affine, stored as SMatrix{4,3,Float32,12} so linear indices run along rows. Byte-identical to VkTransformMatrixKHR; Metal repacks it into its column-major descriptor layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.Mat3x4f"},{"category":"type","location":"index.html#Raycore.TriangleLeaves","page":"Home","text":"A BLAS's leaves: each holds its triangle's three vertices in its box slots (BVH2IL).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.TriangleLeaves"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Adapt.adapt_structure-Tuple{Any, StaticTLAS}","page":"Home","text":"Adapt.adapt_structure(to, tlas::StaticTLAS) -> StaticTLAS\n\nAdapt StaticTLAS arrays. If already isbits, returns as-is. Otherwise adapts each array (CLArray → CLDeviceVector).\n\nNote: StaticTLAS should come from adapting a mutable TLAS, where BLASes already have isbits device pointers. Use TLAS(items) to create a mutable TLAS that properly manages GPU array lifetimes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Adapt.adapt_structure"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Adapt.adapt_structure-Tuple{Any, TLAS}","page":"Home","text":"Adapt.adapt_structure(to, tlas::TLAS) -> StaticTLAS\n\nReturn tlas.static_tlas after a sync! to make sure it reflects any pending mutation. sync! is the single owner of that field; this function just reads it. On a clean TLAS, sync! is a true no-op — no GPU synchronize, no allocations — so this is cheap to call per dispatch. Do call it per dispatch, and do NOT cache the return.\n\ntlas.static_tlas is in kernel-ready isbits form on tlas.backend. The to argument is checked: passing a KA.Backend other than tlas.backend errors loudly. Cross-backend conversion is not supported — build the TLAS on the same backend you intend to dispatch kernels against.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Adapt.adapt_structure"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Base.append!-Tuple{MultiTypeSet, Any}","page":"Home","text":"append!(dhv::MultiTypeSet, items) -> Vector{SetKey}\n\nAppend every element of items in one pass, returning their keys in order.\n\npush! resizes the GPU slot and writes one element per call, so a loop over N items costs N vkAllocateMemory/vkFreeMemory pairs and N host→device copies. That is fine for the handful of materials a scene declares and catastrophic for anything per-face: registering the area lights of a mesh whose emissive surface is a tessellated sphere (261 120 faces) took ~150 s per mesh, which was ~95 % of the time to build RayDemo/Materials/materials.pbrt.\n\nHere each type slot is resized ONCE and filled with a single copyto!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.append!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Base.delete!-Tuple{MultiTypeSet, SetKey}","page":"Home","text":"delete!(dhv::MultiTypeSet, key::SetKey)\n\nVacate the slot under key: the next push! of an item of its stored type takes it. The item stays where it is until then, so no buffer moves and plans packed with the set stay valid; the caller makes sure nothing reads key any more. An invalid key is a no-op.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.delete!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Base.delete!-Tuple{TLAS, TLASHandle}","page":"Home","text":"delete!(tlas::TLAS, handle::TLASHandle) -> Bool\n\nRemove all instances referenced by the handle. Returns true if successful. The handle becomes invalid after deletion.\n\nNote: The instances array is compacted during sync!, not immediately.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.delete!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Base.eltype-Tuple{TLAS}","page":"Home","text":"Base.eltype(tlas::TraversableTLAS)\n\nGet the element type of primitives stored in the TLAS.  Returns the element type of the first BLAS's primitives; the primtype it was constructed with when it has no BLASes yet.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.eltype"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Base.getindex-Tuple{MultiTypeSet, SetKey}","page":"Home","text":"getindex(dhv::MultiTypeSet, key::SetKey)\n\nThe item stored under key, as the set holds it: its arrays as the TextureRef slots they were uploaded to. Handing parts of it back to update! keeps those slots instead of uploading the arrays again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.getindex"},{"category":"function","location":"index.html#Base.push!","page":"Home","text":"push!(tlas::TLAS, mesh::GeometryBasics.Mesh, transform::Mat4f=Mat4f(I);\n      instance_id::UInt32=UInt32(0), instance_mask::UInt8=0xff, opaque=true) -> TLASHandle\n\nAdd a GeometryBasics.Mesh to the TLAS. Per-face metadata is read from the mesh's face_meta attribute (if present). If no face_meta attribute exists, each triangle gets UInt32(face_idx) as metadata.\n\ninstance_id is forwarded to the InstanceDescriptor.  It defaults to 0 (inherit from triangle metadata); pass a nonzero value to override the per-triangle interface — see InstanceDescriptor for semantics.\n\ninstance_mask is the instance's cull mask: a ray traced with mask m sees it only when instance_mask & m != 0 (see closest_hit). The same keyword, with the same meaning, as a hardware structure's push!.\n\nopaque = false makes transparent_hits record the mesh's hits rather than stop at them; see set_opaque!.\n\nReturns a stable handle for later reference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.push!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Base.push!-Tuple{TLAS, GeometryBasics.Mesh, AbstractVector{StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{4, 4, Float32, 16}}}","page":"Home","text":"push!(tlas::TLAS, mesh::GeometryBasics.Mesh, transforms::AbstractVector{Mat4f};\n      instance_ids = nothing, instance_mask::UInt8 = 0xff, opaque = true) -> TLASHandle\n\nAdd a GeometryBasics.Mesh to the TLAS with multiple transforms (instancing). Builds BLAS once, creates length(transforms) InstanceDescriptors.\n\ninstance_ids (if given) must match length(transforms) and supplies the per-instance interface override.  When nothing, every instance gets 0 (inherit from triangle metadata). Every instance gets instance_mask and opaque.\n\nReturns a stable handle for later reference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.push!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.aabb_overlaps-NTuple{4, GeometryBasics.Point{3, Float32}}","page":"Home","text":"Test if two AABBs overlap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.aabb_overlaps"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.allocate_empty_blas_array-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"Helper to create initial empty BLAS array placeholder.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.allocate_empty_blas_array"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.append_blas!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"Append a single isbits BLAS to blasarray using GPU-friendly append!. Returns the (possibly new) blasarray.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.append_blas!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.append_instances_with_handle!-Tuple{TLAS, AbstractVector{InstanceDescriptor}}","page":"Home","text":"Internal: append the given instance descriptors to tlas.instances, register a handle for the appended range, give it the records of the same instances (instance_buffer), and return it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.append_instances_with_handle!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.build_and_append_blas!-Tuple{TLAS, GeometryBasics.Mesh}","page":"Home","text":"Internal: decompose a GB.Mesh, build a BLAS, append it to the TLAS, and return the new BLAS's 1-based index.  Shared by every push! variant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.build_and_append_blas!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.build_flat_blas_arrays!-Tuple{TLAS}","page":"Home","text":"build_flat_blas_arrays!(tlas::TLAS)\n\nBuild concatenated flat arrays from individual BLAS GPU arrays and store them in tlas._flat_blas_nodes, tlas._flat_blas_prims, tlas._flat_blas_descs.\n\nThis avoids storing device pointers in GPU buffers (which fails on Metal). Instead, traversal kernels use BLASDescriptor offsets to index into the flat arrays.\n\nThe flat arrays are MtlVector/CuVector etc., kept alive by the TLAS. During adapt, they are converted to isbits device pointers for kernels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.build_flat_blas_arrays!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.build_tlas_topology-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"build_tlas_topology(blas_array, instances, backend) -> (nodes, root_aabb)\n\nInternal: Build TLAS BVH topology (Morton codes, sorting, tree construction, refit). Returns (nodes, rootaabb). Only accesses blasarray for root_aabb (inline data).\n\ninstances must already be on the backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.build_tlas_topology"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.calculate_morton_codes_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"GPU kernel: Parallel dispatch for Morton code calculation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.calculate_morton_codes_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.calculate_tlas_morton_codes_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"GPU kernel: Calculate Morton codes for TLAS instances.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.calculate_tlas_morton_codes_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.check_args_limits-Union{Tuple{Type{Args}}, Tuple{Args}} where Args<:Tuple","page":"Home","text":"check_args_limits(::Type{Args}) where Args\n\nCompile-time check that argument types don't exceed compiler limits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.check_args_limits"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.check_no_capture-Union{Tuple{Type{F}}, Tuple{F}} where F","page":"Home","text":"check_no_capture(::Type{F}) where F\n\nCompile-time check that function type F has no captured variables. Any closure field indicates a captured variable which should be passed as an argument instead. Core.Box fields are especially problematic (heap-allocated, type-unstable).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.check_no_capture"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.clz32-Tuple{UInt32}","page":"Home","text":"Count leading zeros (clz) for 32-bit integer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.clz32"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.collapse_bvh2_to_bvh4-Tuple{AbstractVector{BVHNode2}, AbstractVector{<:Triangle}, Int32}","page":"Home","text":"Collapse a BVH2 into a BVH4.\n\nThis is a sequential CPU implementation for simplicity. A GPU version would use work queues similar to HIPRT.\n\nThe key insight is that we need to:\n\nCollect up to 4 subtrees at each BVH4 interior node\nFor leaf subtrees, create BVH4 leaf nodes pointing to primitives\nFor interior subtrees, recursively process them\nFix up child pointers to point to BVH4 indices (not BVH2 indices)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.collapse_bvh2_to_bvh4"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.collide_instances_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"collide_instances_kernel!\n\nFor each TLAS leaf (instance), traverse the TLAS tree to find overlapping instances. Two-pass: when contacts is nothing, only counts. When not nothing, writes pairs.\n\nUses stack-based depth-first traversal, same pattern as closest_hit but testing AABB-AABB overlap instead of ray-AABB intersection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.collide_instances_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.compact_instances!-Tuple{TLAS}","page":"Home","text":"Internal: Compact instances array by removing deleted handles, and compact BLASes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.compact_instances!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.compute_instance_aabbs_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"GPU kernel: Compute world AABBs for all instances, storing min/max points separately.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.compute_instance_aabbs_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.coordinate_system-Tuple{GeometryBasics.Vec{3, Float32}}","page":"Home","text":"coordinate_system(n::Vec3f) -> (tangent, bitangent)\n\nAn orthonormal basis around the unit vector n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.coordinate_system"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.create_leaf_nodes_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"GPU kernel: Parallel leaf node creation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.create_leaf_nodes_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.create_tlas_leaf_nodes_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"GPU kernel: Create TLAS leaf nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.create_tlas_leaf_nodes_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.delta-Tuple{Int32, Int32, AbstractVector{UInt32}, Int32}","page":"Home","text":"Compute longest common prefix (LCP) of Morton codes. Uses index fallback when codes are identical.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.delta"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.emit_topology_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"GPU kernel: Parallel topology emission.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.emit_topology_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.empty_bvh4_node-Tuple{}","page":"Home","text":"Create an empty/invalid BVH4 node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.empty_bvh4_node"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.expand_bits-Tuple{UInt32}","page":"Home","text":"3-dilate bits for Morton code (spreads bits by factor of 3).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.expand_bits"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.face_forward-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"Flip normal n so that it lies in the same hemisphere as v.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.face_forward"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.fast_intersect_bbox-Tuple{GeometryBasics.Point{3, Float32}, GeometryBasics.Vec{3, Float32}, Bounds3, Float32, Float32}","page":"Home","text":"fast_intersect_bbox(ray_o, ray_inv_d, bbox, t_min, t_max) -> (entry_t, exit_t)\n\nFast ray-AABB intersection using slab method. Returns parametric distances to entry and exit points. Matches HLSL fastintersectbbox.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.fast_intersect_bbox"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.fast_intersect_bbox4-Tuple{GeometryBasics.Point{3, Float32}, GeometryBasics.Vec{3, Float32}, BVHNode4, Int64, Float32, Float32}","page":"Home","text":"fast_intersect_bbox4(ray_o, ray_inv_d, node, child_idx, t_min, t_max) -> (hit, t_entry)\n\nTest ray against one child AABB of a BVH4 node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.fast_intersect_bbox4"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.fast_intersect_triangle-Tuple{GeometryBasics.Point{3, Float32}, GeometryBasics.Vec{3, Float32}, GeometryBasics.Point{3, Float32}, GeometryBasics.Point{3, Float32}, GeometryBasics.Point{3, Float32}, Float32, Float32}","page":"Home","text":"fast_intersect_triangle(ray_o, ray_d, v0, v1, v2, t_min, closest_t) -> (hit, t, u, v)\n\nMöller-Trumbore ray-triangle intersection test. Matches HLSL reference implementation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.fast_intersect_triangle"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.fill_bvhnode2_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"GPU kernel: Fill array with a value (workaround for OpenCL's fill! not supporting structs).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.fill_bvhnode2_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.find_span_for_node-Tuple{Int32, AbstractVector{UInt32}, Int32}","page":"Home","text":"Find the span of primitives covered by this internal node (Karras 2012).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.find_span_for_node"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.find_split_in_span-Tuple{Int32, Int32, AbstractVector{UInt32}, Int32}","page":"Home","text":"Find the split position within a span (Karras 2012).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.find_split_in_span"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.flatten_blas-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"flatten_blas(blases, bounds) -> (nodes, primitives, descriptors)\n\nThe nodes and the primitives of blases, each concatenated into one array like the first BLAS's, and per BLAS a descriptor of its offsets into them and its box bounds[i].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.flatten_blas"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.foreach_element-Union{Tuple{Textures}, Tuple{Data}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, StaticMultiTypeSet{Data, Textures}, Vararg{Any}}} where {F, Data<:Tuple, Textures}","page":"Home","text":"foreach_element(f, smv::StaticMultiTypeSet, args...)\n\nExecute function f for each element in the StaticMultiTypeSet, passing additional args. The function is called as f(element, linear_idx, args...) where element has a concrete type and linear_idx is the 1-based linear index across all type slots.\n\nUses compile-time unrolled loops for type stability. The function f must not capture variables - pass all data as args.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.foreach_element"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.gather_children_bvh2-Tuple{UInt32, AbstractVector{BVHNode2}, Int32}","page":"Home","text":"Gather up to 4 children from a subtree of the binary BVH. Performs a BFS-like traversal to collect the 4 best children.\n\nReturns: (childindices, childaabbs, childcount, isleaf_flags)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.gather_children_bvh2"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.get_isbits_ptr_type-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{KernelAbstractions.POCL.POCLKernels.POCLBackend, Type{T}}} where T","page":"Home","text":"Get the isbits pointer type for a given element type and backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.get_isbits_ptr_type"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.get_node_aabb4-Tuple{BVHNode4}","page":"Home","text":"Get the node's total AABB (union of all valid children).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.get_node_aabb4"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.hit_barycentric-Tuple{AbstractAdaptedAccel, HitRecord}","page":"Home","text":"hit_barycentric(adapted, rec::HitRecord) -> SVector{3,Float32}\n\nThe barycentrics of the hit on hit_primitive's primitive. A structure with generated hits answers those itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.hit_barycentric"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.hit_result-Tuple{AbstractAdaptedAccel, HitRecord}","page":"Home","text":"hit_result(adapted, rec::HitRecord) -> (hit, primitive, t, bary, instance_id)\n\nA record as the tuple closest_hit and any_hit return.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.hit_result"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.holds_texture-Tuple{Type{<:TextureRef}}","page":"Home","text":"Whether a value of type T holds a texture slot anywhere inside it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.holds_texture"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.instance_world_aabb-Tuple{InstanceDescriptor, AbstractVector}","page":"Home","text":"The world-space box of instance inst: its BLAS's box with all 8 corners transformed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.instance_world_aabb"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.instancecount-Tuple{TLAS, TLASHandle}","page":"Home","text":"instancecount(accel, handle) -> Int\n\nHow many instances handle names in accel; an ArgumentError for a handle it does not know or has deleted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.instancecount"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.instanceranges-Tuple{TLAS}","page":"Home","text":"instanceranges(accel) -> iterator of (handle, range)\n\nEvery live batch of accel with the positions of its instances in the structure's instance order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.instanceranges"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.instancerecords-Tuple{AbstractVector, Union{Nothing, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}, UInt8}","page":"Home","text":"instancerecords(transforms, ids, mask) -> Vector{InstanceRecord}\n\nThe records a push! of a batch of transforms (Mat4fs) makes, with custom indices ids (nothing for all zero) and one mask. An ArgumentError when ids does not have one entry per transform, before any geometry is built.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.instancerecords"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.intersect_all_children4-Tuple{BVHNode4, GeometryBasics.Vec{3, Float32}, GeometryBasics.Point{3, Float32}, Float32, Float32}","page":"Home","text":"intersect_all_children4(node, ray_inv_d, ray_o, t_min, t_max) -> sorted hits\n\nTest ray against all 4 children AABBs and return sorted by distance. Returns up to 4 (childidx, tentry) pairs, sorted near-to-far.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.intersect_all_children4"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.intersect_internal_node-Tuple{BVHNode2, GeometryBasics.Vec{3, Float32}, GeometryBasics.Point{3, Float32}, Float32, Float32}","page":"Home","text":"intersect_internal_node(node, ray_inv_d, ray_o, t_min, t_max) -> (near_child, far_child)\n\nTest ray against internal node's two children AABBs. Returns ordered children indices (near first, far second). INVALID_NODE if child is not intersected. Matches HLSL IntersectInternalNode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.intersect_internal_node"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.intersect_leaf_node-Tuple{BVHNode2, GeometryBasics.Vec{3, Float32}, GeometryBasics.Point{3, Float32}, Float32, Float32}","page":"Home","text":"intersect_leaf_node(node, ray_d, ray_o, t_min, closest_t) -> (hit, t, u, v)\n\nTest ray against triangle stored in leaf node. Returns hit status and intersection parameters. Matches HLSL IntersectLeafNode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.intersect_leaf_node"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.intersect_p-Tuple{Bounds3, Raycore.AbstractRay, GeometryBasics.Vec{3, Float32}, GeometryBasics.Point3{UInt8}}","page":"Home","text":"dirisnegative: 1 – false, 2 – true\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.intersect_p"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.iota_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"GPU kernel: Fill array with sequential indices [1, 2, 3, ..., n].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.iota_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.is_interior-Tuple{BVHNode2}","page":"Home","text":"Check if a node is an interior node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.is_interior"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.is_leaf-Tuple{BVHNode2}","page":"Home","text":"Check if a node is a leaf node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.is_leaf"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.isbits_blas-Tuple{Any, BLAS}","page":"Home","text":"blas with its arrays as isbits device pointers, the form blas_array holds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.isbits_blas"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.lbvh_topology-Tuple{Any, Any, Integer}","page":"Home","text":"The node array of an LBVH over n leaves, with the radix tree over the sorted Morton codes (Karras 2012) and the parent links in place; the leaves are the caller's to fill.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.lbvh_topology"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.leaf_index-Tuple{Integer, Int32}","page":"Home","text":"Compute leaf node index from primitive index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.leaf_index"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.maximum_extent-Tuple{Bounds3}","page":"Home","text":"Return index of the longest axis. Useful for deciding which axis to subdivide, when building ray-tracing acceleration structures.\n\n1 - x, 2 - y, 3 - z.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.maximum_extent"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.meshtriangles-Tuple{GeometryBasics.Mesh}","page":"Home","text":"meshtriangles(mesh) -> Vector{<:Triangle}\nmeshtriangles(Tri, mesh) -> Vector{Tri}\n\nThe nondegenerate triangles of mesh in face order, each carrying its face's face_meta, or its face index when the mesh has none: dropping a degenerate face does not renumber the others.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.meshtriangles"},{"category":"function","location":"index.html#Raycore.miss_primitive","page":"Home","text":"miss_primitive(adapted) -> primitive\n\nWhat closest_hit returns as the primitive of a miss: the empty one of the structure's primitive type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.miss_primitive"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.morton_code_30bit-Tuple{GeometryBasics.Point{3, Float32}}","page":"Home","text":"Calculate 30-bit Morton code from normalized 3D point [0,1]³. Interleaves x,y,z bits to create space-filling Z-curve ordering.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.morton_code_30bit"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.morton_extent-Tuple{Bounds3}","page":"Home","text":"The scene box's extent for Morton codes, at least 1e-6 on every axis: a flat mesh or a row of instances has zero extent on an axis, and the codes divide by it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.morton_extent"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.node_aabb-Tuple{Any, BVHNode2, Bool}","page":"Home","text":"The box around node: the union of an interior node's two child boxes, else its leaf's box.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.node_aabb"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.offset-Tuple{Bounds3, GeometryBasics.Point{3, Float32}}","page":"Home","text":"Get offset of a point from the minimum point of the bounds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.offset"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.rebuild_bvh!-Tuple{TLAS}","page":"Home","text":"Internal: Compact deleted instances and rebuild TLAS BVH.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.rebuild_bvh!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.rebuild_static_tlas!-Tuple{TLAS}","page":"Home","text":"rebuild_static_tlas!(tlas::TLAS)\n\nBuild a fresh StaticTLAS from the current tlas.nodes / tlas.instances / flat-BLAS arrays and store it on tlas.static_tlas. Called by sync! — not a public API; consumers read tlas.static_tlas or Adapt.adapt(backend, tlas).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.rebuild_static_tlas!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.record-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{TransparentHits{N}, HitRecord, Integer}} where N","page":"Home","text":"record(th::TransparentHits, rec::HitRecord, limit) -> TransparentHits\n\nth with the hit rec counted and kept, if among the nearest N; past limit counted hits, blocked. A primitive already kept is not counted again: a traversal may offer one twice. What every structure's transparent_hits calls at a hit it does not stop at, as Cycles' bvh_shadow_all_anyhit_filter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.record"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.refit_lbvh!-Tuple{Any, Integer, Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"The interior boxes of the LBVH nodes, refit bottom-up from its n filled leaves, which hold leaves; returns the root's box.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.refit_lbvh!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.refit_tlas!-Tuple{TLAS}","page":"Home","text":"refit_tlas!(tlas::TLAS)\n\nRefit the TLAS after instance transforms have been updated. Updates leaf AABBs from instance transforms and propagates changes up the tree.\n\nThis is much faster than rebuilding the TLAS from scratch when only transforms change. Operates directly on the backend arrays stored in the TLAS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.refit_tlas!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.requirerange-Tuple{TLAS, TLASHandle, Any}","page":"Home","text":"The positions in tlas.instances of the instances of handle; an ArgumentError naming operation for a handle it does not know or has deleted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.requirerange"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.resized-Tuple{AbstractVector, Tuple{Int64}}","page":"Home","text":"resized(arr, dims) -> array\n\narr at the new shape: the same array, resized in place, for a vector; a fresh array of arr's type for any other shape, whose contents the caller writes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.resized"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.rootbounds-Tuple{BLAS}","page":"Home","text":"rootbounds(x) -> Bounds3\n\nThe bounds of what an instance instances, in its own space. The TLAS management kernels read nothing else of a BLAS, so they take either the BLASes themselves (the dynamic TLAS keeps a device array of isbits ones) or only their root bounds (build_tlas, whose BLASes are host structs of arrays, which no kernel can take).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.rootbounds"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.rotate-Tuple{Real, GeometryBasics.Vec{3, Float32}}","page":"Home","text":"rotate(θ, axis) -> Transformation\n\nThe rotation by θ degrees about axis, see rotation_matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.rotate"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.rotation_matrix-Tuple{GeometryBasics.Vec{3, Float32}, Float32, Float32}","page":"Home","text":"rotation_matrix(axis::Vec3f, s::Float32, c::Float32) -> Mat3f\n\nThe rotation about the unit vector axis by the angle whose sine is s and cosine c (Rodrigues' formula), written column by column: Mat3f(...) fills column-major.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.rotation_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.rotation_matrix-Tuple{Real, GeometryBasics.Vec{3, Float32}}","page":"Home","text":"rotation_matrix(θ, axis) -> Mat3f\n\nThe rotation by θ degrees about axis, counter-clockwise looking down the axis, like pbrt's Rotate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.rotation_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.safe_invdir-Tuple{GeometryBasics.Vec{3, Float32}}","page":"Home","text":"safe_invdir(d::Vec3f) -> Vec3f\n\nSafe ray direction inversion that avoids division by zero. Clamps near-zero components to ±1e-5. Matches HLSL reference implementation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.safe_invdir"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.set_parent_pointers_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"GPU kernel: Parallel parent pointer assignment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.set_parent_pointers_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.sort_morton-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"The permutation that sorts codes, and the sorted codes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.sort_morton"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.tlas_node_aabb-Tuple{BVHNode2}","page":"Home","text":"The box of TLAS node node, as (min, max).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.tlas_node_aabb"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.to_isbits_blas-Tuple{Any, BLAS, Vector{Raycore.BLASArrays}}","page":"Home","text":"Convert a BLAS with backend arrays to an isbits BLAS with device pointers.\n\nAppends a BLASArrays(nodes, primitives) to blas_storage so the backing buffers outlive every isbits pointer that references them.\n\nNote: The isbits BLAS is only used by management kernels that read root_aabb (inline data). For traversal, StaticTLAS uses flat arrays with offset-based indexing instead (see BLASDescriptor).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.to_isbits_blas"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.transform-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{4, 3, Float32, 12}, Triangle}","page":"Home","text":"transform(m::Mat3x4f, t::Triangle) -> Triangle\n\nThe triangle moved by instance transform m: vertices as points, tangents as directions, normals through transform_normal. Used to hand a hit triangle to the caller in the space of the ray, not of its BLAS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.transform"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.transform_normal-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{4, 3, Float32, 12}, GeometryBasics.Vec{3, Float32}}","page":"Home","text":"transform_normal(m::Mat3x4f, n::Vec3f) -> Vec3f\n\nSurface normal n under m, normalized. Uses the cofactor of the linear part, which is its inverse transpose up to the determinant: right under non-uniform scale, and no inverse to compute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.transform_normal"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.traverse-Union{Tuple{E}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Any, StaticTLAS, Raycore.AbstractRay, Integer}, Tuple{F, Any, StaticTLAS, Raycore.AbstractRay, Integer, E}} where {F, E}","page":"Home","text":"traverse(visit, state, tlas::StaticTLAS, ray::AbstractRay, mask, enters = everything) -> state\n\nWalk the two-level BVH along ray and hand every triangle it intersects to visit(state, t, u, v, instance, primitive, custom) -> (state, t_max, done): the hit's distance and barycentrics, the instance's index (0-based) and the primitive's in its BLAS, and the instance's custom index. The walk goes on with t_max as the ray's far end, until done or the end of the BVH, and returns the last state. What closest_hit_record, any_hit_record and transparent_hits differ in is their visit and which instances they enter.\n\nmask is the ray's cull mask: an instance takes part only when its own mask (instance_mask at push!) shares a bit with it, so 0xff sees every instance and 0x00 none. The same rule as the hardware structures, which only count the low 8 bits, as this does. Of those, the walk enters only the instances enters(instance_descriptor) accepts.\n\nThe state lives in registers for the whole walk, so it must stay small: four recorded hits carried through it made a shadow ray three times slower on an RTX 4000 Ada, with nothing ever recorded.\n\nAlgorithm (HLSL TraceRays):\n\nTraverse TLAS to find candidate instances\nTransform ray to local space\nTraverse BLAS for geometry intersection\nReport each hit in the ray's own parameter\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.traverse"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.update_instance_transform!-Tuple{TLAS, Integer, StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{4, 3, Float32, 12}}","page":"Home","text":"update_instance_transform!(tlas::TLAS, instance_idx::Integer, transform)\n\nUpdate the transform of the instance at position instance_idx of tlas.instances, in the instance and in its batch's record (instance_buffer) alike, keeping its id and mask. The next sync! refits the BVH over it. An ArgumentError for a position no live instance has.\n\nArguments\n\ntlas: The TLAS to update\ninstance_idx: 1-based index of the instance to update\ntransform: New local-to-world transform — Mat4f or canonical Vulkan row-major 3×4 (Mat3x4f)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.update_instance_transform!"},{"category":"function","location":"index.html#Raycore.update_instance_transforms!","page":"Home","text":"update_instance_transforms!(tlas::TLAS, transforms, n_to_update[, first_idx = 1])\n\nThe flat-index form of update_transforms!: the instances first_idx:first_idx + n_to_update - 1 of tlas.instances, which must be exactly one batch, written in the instances and in the batch's records alike. Written once for every structure, on its instanceranges. Any other range is an ArgumentError: a range that splits a batch has no records of its own to write. Zero instances is a no-op.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.update_instance_transforms!"},{"category":"method","location":"index.html#Raycore.zero_struct-Union{Tuple{Type{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Home","text":"zero_struct(::Type{T}) -> T\n\nField-wise zero-initialise a POD struct T.  Recursive over fieldtype(T, i) so nested structs (e.g. Triangle{TriangleMeta} where TriangleMeta has UInt32 fields) work without requiring zero(T) to be defined on the outer type.  Falls back to zero(T) for primitive leaves.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Raycore.zero_struct"}]
}
